namedrockunit:ATG
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/AND"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/BA"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/RY"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "[Obsolete: use ATGK] Sandstone predominates, interbedded with clast-supported conglomerate with well-rounded boulders, cobbles and pebbles predominantly of lava, psammite and quartzite in the north-east and adjacent to the Highland Boundary Fault. Siltstone and mudstone are interbedded with the sandstone, particularly in Dundee area. Piles of andesitic, basaltic and rhyolitic lavas, locally very thick, are interbedded with conglomerate and sandstone in many areas. Lenses and thicker formations of volcaniclastic sandstone are interbedded with conglomerate in the Stonehaven area and are associated with lavas elsewhere."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Diachronous, rising to higher levels towards the south-west. In the type area it lies at top of highest lava of Crawton Volcanic Formation, where this is overlain by clast-supported conglomerate. Elsewhere its base is taken to lie at the eroded top of the Lintrathen Porphyry or its presumed correlatives (cf. Paterson and Harris, 1969). Where these horizons are absent, on sheets 66E and 67, the base has been taken at the base of the lowest of the series of andesitic lavas that characterise the Arbuthnott Group in that area."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DO> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.19e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Variable; the complete group ranges in thickness from 880-1350m in the Stonehaven area, while in the Stirling area, the Ochil Volcanic Formation alone reaches a maximum of 2400m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Diachronous, taken to lie at top of thick pile of andesitic lavas in south-west (Ochil Volcanic Formation) and north-east (Montrose Volcanic Formation) of crop. In Dundee area, taken at level of top of highest fine-grained horizon (`fish-bed') at the top of the Dundee Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DP> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.105e+02 ;
    lexicon:isObsoleteEquivalentOf namedrockunit:ATGK ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/deprecated> ;
    skos:altLabel "Arbuthnott-Garvock Group"@en ;
    skos:definition "Obsolete name. Sandstone with subsidiary andesite, basalt, conglomerate and siltstone and trace mudstone and rhyolite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Lochkovian Age - to - Pragian Age (Devonian Period) (419-410.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ATG"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arbuthnott Group [Obsolete: use ATGK]"@en ;
.

