namedrockunit:BECB
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SC"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """A lenticular bed of clay and clayey sand occurring locally within the Becton Sand. \r
\r
At outcrop, it consists of highly bioturbated, carbonaceous, clayey, very fine-grained sand and silt that passes eastward into clay-free sand. In the Ramnor Inclosure Borehole it consists of shelly sandy clay and clayey very fine-grained sand and silt that is lithologically similar to the Barton Clay (BAC). In the borehole there is a basal bed approx. 1.1 m thick of gravelly clay with shell debris. \r
\r
In the Ramnor Inclosure Borehole the Becton Bunny Member yielded a moderately diverse fauna, mainly of bivalves and gastropods, indicative of shallow marine conditions."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/G> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 6.6e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/G> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The Becton Bunny Member is thickest between Exbury and the Ramnor Inclosure Borehole. In the Ramnor Inclosure Borehole it is about 11.8 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/G> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 2.304e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:BECS ;
    skos:definition "Clay and clayey sand. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Paleogene Period (66-23.04 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "BECB"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Becton Bunny Member"@en ;
.

