namedrockunit:CCM
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/C> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CASLST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DOLMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Interbedded grey mudstones and thin- to medium-bedded skeletal packstones deposited in a mid-shelf environment."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Where back barrier mudstones, calcite mudstones and calcisiltites occupy the uppermost part of the underlying Castell Coch Limestone Formation, the base of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation is taken at the sharp incoming of marine, interbedded grey mudstones and packstones. The contact is commonly erosional and overlain by a packstone or grainstone bed rich in intraclasts of the underlying back barrier mudstones. Where the uppermost part of the Castell Coch Limestone Formation comprises barrier grainstones, the base of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation is taken at the sharp incoming of interbedded grey mudstones and packstones."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CF> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.593e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CF> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "60m in the south of the Vale of Glamorgan, thinning northwards to 10m on the north crop of the South Wales Coalfield [Abergavenny]."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The contact of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation with the overlying Barry Harbour Limestone Formation is gradational. In the uppermost part of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation, the proportion of packstone beds increases to exceed that of mudstone. The top of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation is taken at the top of the last significant mudstone bed above which the succession becomes dominated by stacked fine and coarse skeletal packstones with scattered thin beds and partings of calcareous mudstone. In the north crop of the South Wakes Coalfield, the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation is overlain by the Sychnant Dolomite Formation or the Pwll-y-cwm Oolite Formation. The junction with the Sychnant Dolomite Formation is gradational. Thin beds of calcite mudstone and dolomitic limestone appear in the uppermost part of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation mudstone/packstone sequence. The top of the formation is taken at the incoming of abundant, thin-bedded, fine-grained, dolomitised skeletal packstones of the Sychnant Dolomite Formation. The contact with the Pwll-y-cwm Oolite Formation is sharp and taken at the incoming of thick-bedded pale grey oolitic grainstone above the interbedded mudstones and packstones of the Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CF> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.467e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:AVO ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone with subsidiary limestone and trace calcareous siltstone and dolomitic limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Courceyan Substage (Carboniferous Period) (359.3-346.7 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "CCM"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cwmyniscoy Mudstone Formation"@en ;
.

