namedrockunit:CRUI
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/K> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Cruiser Formation consists predominantly of mid grey to dark grey mudstones. These may be carbonaceous (with plant fragments), micaceous, pyritic, and interbedded with occasional thin siltstones, fine quartz sandstones and limestones. Much of the mudstone is non-calcareous. Within the Cruiser Formation in 205/10-2B, there are numerous Tertiary or older intrusions."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "In the centre of the Faeroe-Shetland Basin, the base of the Cruiser Formation has not been reached and the lower boundary cannot yet be defined in this area. Along the margins of the basin or on intrabasin highs, the Cruiser Formation either rests unconformably on older strata (e.g. Kimmeridge Clay Formation in 206/5-1) or on the sandstones and conglomerates of the Royal Sovereign Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.132e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "It is 772 m thick in the type well. It reaches a maximum drilled thickness of 1154.5 m in 214/29-1Z, although there are no wireline logs covering the basal 423 m in this well, and the base of the formation was not reached."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Cruiser Formation is normally defined by a downward change from the interbedded limestones, siltstones and mudstones of the Svarte Formation into more uniform mudstones. It is usually marked on wireline logs by a downward change from the more irregular and generally higher gammaray and sonic velocities associated with the Svarte Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.005e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CRKN ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone with subsidiary limestone, sandstone and siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Albian Age (Cretaceous Period) (113.2-100.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "CRUI"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cruiser Formation"@en ;
.

