namedrockunit:ESKB
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CLCRET"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/COAL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Interbedded red mudstone (claystones and siltstones), fine- to medium-grained sandstones, calcrete palaeosols, thin beds of 'Spirorbis' limestone and Estheria-bearing mudstones. Sparse thin coals and grey mudstones are present in the lower part of the formation; some of these coals have been oxidised and altered to limestone (Jones and Holliday, 2006; Jones et al., 2011; see also Mykura, 1960). Mudstones form 60-70 per cent of the formation, with sandstones forming most of the rest. \r
\r
Deposition on an alluvial plain with lakes. The flood plain alternating from poorly to well drained. \r
\r
Late Bolsovian (Westphalian C) to Asturian (Westphalian D)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the formation was not identified at outcrop and is defined in boreholes. The boundary is taken at the first major red-bed strata overlying the grey mudstone-dominated Pennine Upper Coal Measures Formation. It is a conformable, gradational boundary. In the Becklees Borehole the base of the formation (at 816.8 m depth) is defined by the last significant downhole occurrence of primary red-bed lithologies. In this instance it comprises 1.3 m of mottled reddish brown and greenish grey silty mudstone with abundant carbonate nodules. These nodules are interpreted as pedogenic calcrete glaebules and represent the first good evidence for the development of primary red-bed conditions. A limited number of red lithologies are known below 816.8 m depth, but it is not known whether these represent primary red-beds or could be linked to a later secondary reddening event. The gradational boundary to the formation is well demonstrated by the reversion higher in the borehole to grey lithologies. This includes a prominent coal (the High Coal), which is about 1 m in thickness and must represent a significant amount of time in which reducing conditions prevailed. In the Forge Diamond Bore the base is taken at the change from pale greenish mudstones to red and green mudstones at 235.5 m depth. The lower boundary is difficult to pick in uncored boreholes, but analysis of cored boreholes shows that close to the base of the formation the distinctive High Coal is typically present, and can be identified from suitable geophysical logs (e.g. sonic, density). It is suggested that the position of this coal be taken as the base of the formation where core data are absent."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CC> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.1475e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "In the Forge Diamond, Becklees and Glenzierfoot boreholes the formation is about 163 m, 164 m and 146 m thick respectively. In the Rowanburnfoot Bore (BGS Registration Number NY47NW/27) [NY 41031 75743] it is 175 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the formation is taken at the base of the lowermost sandstone bed of the Canonbie Bridge Sandstone Formation. This is typically a thick (20-30 m) multistorey sandstone complex. The abrupt junction can be examined at outcrop at [NY 39194 76953] and has also been proven in numerous boreholes, including the Becklees Borehole (cored depth: 653 m; geophysical log depth: 656 m), and those at Broadmeadows (BGS Registration Number NY37NE/15) [NY 37646 76265] (geophysical log depth: 176.6 m) and Glenzierfoot (BGS Registration Number NY37SE/2) [NY 36514 74275] (geophysical log depth: 368.2 m)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CAS> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.075e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:WAWK ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone with subsidiary sandstone and trace calcrete, coal and limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Bolsovian Substage - to - Asturian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (314.75-307.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ESKB"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eskbank Wood Formation"@en ;
.

