namedrockunit:FRPI
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/P"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/TUFA"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Floodplain alluvium and river terrace deposits. The floodplain alluvium comprises  clay and silt overbank deposits, with local peat, overlying sand and gravel. The surviving river terrace deposits are largely gravel with interstitial sand. The major pebble lithologies are flint, quartz and Mesozoic chert. The gravel units were deposited by braided rivers under periglacial conditions."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The fluvial deposits rest unconformably on Chalk Group and Palaeogene strata."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QPE> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.58e+00 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "To 20m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Surface."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QH> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 0e+00 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:SNTCA ;
    skos:definition "Gravel, peat, sand and silt with subsidiary clay and trace tufa. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Early Pleistocene - to - Holocene Epoch (Quaternary Period) (2.58-0 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "FRPI"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Frome-Piddle Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:FRSA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Freshfield Sandstone Member comprises generally soft sandstone to coarse siltstone with sporadic interbedded mudstone. The sandstone is pale red to orange-brown, rarely pale grey or with colourless quartz grains, generally very fine to fine-grained, clean, with subangular to subrounded, moderately well-sorted grains of moderate sphericity set in a variable silica or dolomite cement. Two main facies, not always mutually exclusive, appear to be present: \r
\r
1. Sandstone that generally lacks mudstone partings, has fair visible porosity and possesses a lower sonic velocity (e.g. 110/20-1, 110/13-1) than the Rottington Sandstone Member and the remainder of the Manchester Marls Formation. This type (interpreted here as dominantly aeolian) appears to increase in proportional thickness towards the Llyn-Rossendale Ridge. \r
\r
2. Sandstone that has a poor visible porosity, contains numerous higher gamma mudstone partings and exhibits a linear sonic velocity trace similar to that of the bounding rocks (e.g. 110/11-1, p.l 15; 110/12a-l); this sandstone is interpreted here to comprise mixed aeolian and fluvial components."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """The base of the Freshfield Sandstone Member is placed at the downward change from sandstone to mudstone or siltstone comprising the remainder of the Manchester Marls Formation (e.g. 110/11-1), or, in very limited areas, the remainder of the Barrowmouth Mudstone Formation (110/3b-4). \r
\r
On wireline logs, the base of the member is characterized by the abrupt downward increase in gamma values. The sonic shift at the boundary is variable. Commonly, there is a downward increase in velocity at shallow depths of burial and/or where the member is largely aeolian (e.g. 110/20-1, 110/13-1), but at greater burial depths and/or where fluvial sandstones are an important component, a small downward decrease may be evident (e.g. 110/6b-l)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PUG> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.744e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The Freshfield Sandstone Member thickens progressively southwards from 3 m in 110/3b-4, through 6.5 m in 110/6b-l, to 44 m in 110/20-1. The lateral equivalent onshore is 36.5 m thick in Formby 1 (Kent, 1948)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition """The top of the Freshfield Sandstone Member is placed at the sharp downward change from micaceous sandstone containing numerous thin interbedded mudstones (Rottington Sandstone Member) to cleaner, less micaceous, sandstone of the Freshfield Sandstone Member (e.g. 110/20-1, 110/13-1), generally with fewer mudstones. \r
\r
On wireline logs, the boundary is positioned at the abrupt downward change from high velocity, moderately high gamma sandstone (with numerous thin higher gamma mudstone spikes) to lower gamma sandstone, which commonly exhibits a lower velocity (e.g. 110/20-1, 110/13-1)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PUL> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 2.519e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:MM ;
    skos:definition "Sandstone and siltstone with subsidiary mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Guadalupian Epoch - to - Lopingian Epoch (Permian Period) (274.4-251.9 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "FRSA"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Freshfield Sandstone Member"@en ;
.

