namedrockunit:GRZH
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/ANHY"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Grenzanhydrit Formation consists of white, pale grey and pale orange or red-brown anhydrite that is variously described as soft and amorphous or hard and microcrystalline, sometimes friable, brittle or sucrosic. Locally the Grenzanhydrit Formation contains a unit of red-brown and grey anhydritic claystone and mudstone (e.g. 47/13-2) or white, yellow or red, clear to opaque halite (e.g. 47/15-1)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The lower boundary of the Grenzanhydrit Formation is normally marked by a sharp downward change from white and pale grey anhydrite to halite, K/Mg salts and local, redbrown, saliferous mudstones of the Aller Halite Formation. It coincides with a downward decrease in density and velocity and an increase in gamma values."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PUG> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.744e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Its thickest development is around the northern margin where it reaches 5-8 m (e.g. 36/26-1). Sections of 2-5 m thickness occur over much of the western and southern parts of the basin (e.g. 47/13-2), thinning to 1 m or less close to the southern feather-edge (e.g. 49/26/4). Over the central part of the basin, the formation is usually less than 2 m thick, but is locally absent or below wireline-log resolution."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Grenzanhydrit Formation is normally marked by a sharp downward change from red-brown anhydritic mudstone and siltstone of the Bunter Shale Formation to white and pale grey anhydrite. It coincides with a sharp downward increase in velocity and density and a sharp decrease in gamma values."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PUL> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 2.519e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:ZG ;
    skos:definition "Anhydrite-stone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Guadalupian Epoch - to - Lopingian Epoch (Permian Period) (274.4-251.9 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "GRZH"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Grenzanhydrit Formation"@en ;
.

