namedrockunit:MCBT
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/K> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Macbeth Formation consists of mudstones with interbedded limestones. The mudstones are pale to dark grey, calcareous to non-calcareous and occasionally very silty, micaceous, glauconitic and pyritic. The limestones are chalky and argillaceous, white to pale grey or brownish grey, and locally glauconitic. In the Viking Graben and East Shetland Basin, the limestone units are up to about 60 m thick (e.g. 9/10c-2), but both the proportion of limestone and the thickness of individual limestone beds tend to decrease northwards and away from contemporary structural highs (e.g. 3/10b-1). In the Beryl Embayment and Viking Graben, the formation displays an overall 'barrel-shaped' wireline log signature, with a more calcareous middle section underlain and overlain by more argillaceous deposits (e.g. 9/10c-2). \r
\r
A unit of relatively high-gamma mudstones at the base of the Macbeth Formation is designated the Black Band."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the Macbeth Formation is normally marked by a downward change from essentially non-calcareous mudstones (Black Band) to calcareous mudstones and argillaceous chalky limestones of the Svarte Formation (e.g. 3/29-1 and 9/10c-2). It is marked on wireline logs by a decrease in gamma values and an increase in velocity. Commonly, the Black Band is difficult to recognize (e.g. 3/12-2). Over some structural highs, the Macbeth Formation rests unconformably on very condensed sections of the Cromer Knoll Group or on pre-Cretaceous rocks (e.g. Kimmeridge Clay Formation in 9/19-7Z). In the Beryl Embayment and South Viking Graben, the Macbeth Formation overlies the Hidra Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 9.39e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The Macbeth Formation is up to 175 m thick in the UK North Viking Graben."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Macbeth Formation is normally marked by a downward change from mudstones of the Kyrre Formation to argillaceous chalky limestones and interbedded mudstones. It is marked on wireline logs by a downward decrease in gamma values and an increase in velocity."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 8.94e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:STLN ;
    skos:definition "Limestone and mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Turonian Age (Cretaceous Period) (93.9-89.4 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "MCBT"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Macbeth Formation"@en ;
.

