namedrockunit:STCA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/T> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CHRT"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Stotfield Calcrete Formation is composed mainly of white, pale grey, or occasionally pale brown limestone, which is microcrystalline or cryptocrystalline, and massive or platy. White, pale brown, or bluish grey chert is commonly also encountered in cuttings, and forms the infill of stylolites and veins in well 12/26-1. A few thin beds of pale grey or yellowish orange mudstone are intercalated within the limestones of blocks 12/21 and 12/27."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Stotfield Calcrete Formation rests on Triassic sandstones, siltstones and mudstones of the Lossiehead Formation. Its base can be sharp or transitional, but is generally marked by a downward increase in gamma-ray values and a decrease in both velocity and resistivity. The equivalent boundary at Stotfield is transitional, with highly silicified sandstones grading upwards into an increasingly carbonate-rich rock containing pockets of unaltered sandstone (Naylor et al., 1989)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/TR> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.057e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/TR> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 25 m thick in southwestern Quadrant 12, and more than 10 m thick in most adjacent parts of Quadrants 11 and 18, the formation thins to less than 6 m in the northeast. Equivalent sediments are less than 10 m thick in quarries near Stotfield (Peacock et al., 1968; Naylor et al., 1989)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Stotfield Calcrete Formation is defined by a sharp downward change from alluvial red-beds (Golspie Formation) to limestone, and is marked in all sections by a downhole increase in velocity and resistivity."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/TR> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 2.014e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:HERO ;
    skos:definition "Limestone with subsidiary chert and mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Rhaetian Age (Triassic Period) (205.7-201.4 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "STCA"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stotfield Calcrete Formation"@en ;
.

