        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/BAANDT"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "In 110/2b-10, the Tormentil Volcanics consist of interbedded basalts and basaltic andesites, but possibly include an overlying ?volcaniclastic package of interbedded sandstone, siltstone and mudstone."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """In 110/2b-10, the base of the Tormentil Volcanics is taken at the abrupt downward change from basalt to secondarily reddened mudstone of the Kidston Group (youngest dated interval is Langsettian). Elsewhere in the basin, the unit is likely to rest on lithologically varied Silesian sediments. The zone of secondarily reddened Carboniferous may be thinner than normal beneath the Tormentil Volcanics, as in 110/2b-10. \r
\r
On the wireline log of 110/2b-10, the base of the unit is marked by the abrupt and large downward increase in gamma values, and a small downward decrease on the sonic log. The 13-20 API gamma values displayed by the basalt are similar to those of thick Carboniferous sandstones (e.g. 110/20-1), and conceivably, where basalt rests directly on sandstone, there may be little gamma shift at the base of the unit. There is apparently little downward change in average density values across the boundary."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PA> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.989e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PA> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/U> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "In 110/2b-10, the basalts are 45.5 m thick, and the overlying putative volcaniclastic sediments are 22 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition """In 110/2b-10, the top of the putative volcaniclastic sediments is marked by the sharp downward change from aeolian sandstone of the Collyhurst Sandstone Formation to (?volcaniclastic) mudstone. The top of the lavas is marked by the sharp downward change from siltstone to basalt. \r
\r
On the wireline logs of 110/2b-10, there is a very sharp downward increase in gamma values and coincident sharp downward increase in velocity and density from aeolian sandstone to ?volcaniclastic mudstone. The contact of the ?volcaniclastic sediment on basalt is marked by the abrupt downward decrease in gamma values, and an additional downward increase in velocity and density."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PA> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 2.935e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:APY ;
    skos:definition "Basalt and basaltic-andesite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Asselian Age (Permian Period) (298.9-293.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "TOVO"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tormentil Volcanics"@en ;
.

