namedrockunit:ULT
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/S> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CASLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Upper Leintwardine Formation is transitional between the Lower Leintwardine Formation (LLT) and the Lower Whitcliffe Formation (LWH). It comprises light olive-grey, flaggy calcareous siltstones similar to those of the Lower Leintwardine Formation near Ludlow, but is more irregularly bedded and less calcareous, like the Lower Whitcliffe Formation, farther west, near Downton. \r
\r
As with the Lower Leintwardine Formation (LLT), the coquinoid occurrence of shelly fossils is characteristic. Thin shelly layers are characteristic of the western exposures, but such bands are less common in the east, where the Upper Leintwardine Formation may not be distinguished so readily from the Lower Leintwardine Formation. The most diagnostic feature of the formation is its distinctive arthropod fauna (Shaleria ornatella Association), with the trilobites Calymene puellaris Reed and Encrinurus stubblefieldi Tripp, and the ostracode Neobeyrichia lauensis (Kiesow)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Underlain by the Lower Leintwardine Formation (LLT)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/SD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.25e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/SD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Estimated to be between 1.5 and 5 m thick (Holland et al., 1963; Lawson and White, 1989)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Overlain by the Whitcliffe Formation (WHIT)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/SD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.227e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:definition "Calcareous siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Ludfordian Age (Silurian Period) (425-422.7 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:isTopConceptOf lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ULT"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Upper Leintwardine Formation"@en ;
.

