namedrockunit:UMBED
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Like the Upper Mottled Clay, the Upper Mottled Beds typically consists mainly of clay and silt, with some sand and occasional gravel. The clay of the Upper Mottled Beds tends to be less variable in colour than that of the Lower Mottled Beds. It is typically grey mottled with brown but may also be mottled or multicoloured red or yellow. Beds of dark grey or black clay occur locally. This unit can include channel-form bodies of cross-bedded, fine- to medium-grained sand. It does not contain glauconite. It is locally weakly bioturbated or with a low-diversity non-marine (brackish water) mollusc fauna.\r
Parts of the Upper Mottled Beds that are derived from the Woolwich Formation may include more sand, and relicts of little-altered sediment of the Woolwich Formation."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the Upper Mottled Beds, like the Upper Mottled Clay, is a sharply-defined boundary at base of vari-coloured mottled clay or fine- to medium-grained sand overlying fine-grained sands, silts or clays of the Laminated Beds (Woolwich Formation). Where the Woolwich Formation is absent, the nature of the boundary between the Lower Mottled Beds and the Upper Mottled Beds is not well known."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 5.6e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/U> ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Upper Mottled Beds is the top of the Reading Formation. It is generally sharply defined, at an upward change from vari-coloured mottled clay to glauconitic sands and sandy clays of the Harwich Formation, or to sandy clays of the Walton Member (London Clay Formation), or to shelly grey clays of the Upper Shelly Clay"@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.81e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:RB ;
    skos:definition "Clay and sand. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Ypresian Age (Palaeogene Period) (56-48.1 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "UMBED"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Upper Mottled Beds (Reading Formation)"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:UMCL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Upper Mottled Clay typically consists mainly of clay and silt, with some sand and occasional gravel. The clay of the Upper Mottled Clay tends to be less variable in colour than that of the Lower Mottled Clay. It is typically grey mottled with brown but may also be mottled or multicoloured red or yellow. Beds of dark grey or black clay occur locally. These may be of significance for regional correlation at the interface between the upper and the lower parts of the Reading Formation where the Woolwich Formation is absent. \r
\r
This unit can include channel-form bodies of cross-bedded, fine to medium-grained sand. It is locally weakly bioturbated or with a low-diversity non-marine (brackish water) mollusc fauna. \r
\r
The Upper Mottled Clay comprises fluviatile sediments rather similar to those of the Lower Mottled Clay and is likewise thought to have been deposited under non-marine conditions, on a large coastal plain crossed by rivers. The colour-mottling formed shortly after deposition in a tropical or sub-tropical climate with seasonal rainfall: it is not due to modern weathering processes (Ellison, 1983; Hooker, 1991). \r
\r
Early Eocene (earliest Ypresian). See Reading Formation."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the Upper Mottled Clay is a sharply-defined boundary at base of vari-coloured mottled clay or fine- to medium-grained sand overlying fine-grained sands, silts or clays of the Laminated Beds (Woolwich Formation). Where the Woolwich Formation is absent, the nature of the boundary between the Lower Mottled Clay and the Upper Mottled Clay is not well known. It seems to be marked by a bed of grey clay, or by lignite fragments, or both, at least locally. These presumably form the basal layer of the Upper Mottled Clay."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 5.6e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/U> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 8 m recorded, possibly more to the west of London."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Upper Mottled Clay is the top of the Reading Formation. It is generally sharply defined, at an upward change from vari-coloured mottled clay to glauconitic sands and sandy clays of the Harwich Formation, or to sandy clays of the Walton Member (London Clay Formation), or to shelly grey clays of the Upper Shelly Clay."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.81e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:RB ;
    skos:definition "Clay and silt with subsidiary sand and trace gravel. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Ypresian Age (Palaeogene Period) (56-48.1 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "UMCL"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Upper Mottled Clay"@en ;
.

