        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SEMPEL"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Striped association of semipelite, psammite and siliceous psammite with subordinate quartzite and calc-silicate rock, variably gneissose."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Conformably upon Creag an Loin Psammite Formation (CRAN); marked by incoming of distinctive garnetiferous quartzite and/or semipelite."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1e+03 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AT> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "600-700 metres."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Overlain by Creag Liath Psammite Formation (CGLH); typically marked by a narrow shear zone. Elsewhere overlain by Grampian Shear Zone."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AT> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 7.2e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:GLBA ;
    skos:definition "Psammite and semipelite with subsidiary calcsilicate-rock and quartzite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Tonian Period (Precambrian) (1000-720 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower namedrockunit:BLQA ;
    skos:notation "ANST"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Stac Semipelite and Psammite Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:ANTI
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent namedrockunit:S8 ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Typically very still, dark brown or purplish brown, stony silty sandy clay diamicton with moderately well, to well-dispersed clasts mainly of brown Fell Sandstone, pinkish grey calcareous siltstone (cementstone), limestone and dolerite with other Carboniferous lithologies (yellow sandstone, black mudstone, coal and white silicified sandstone seatearth), purple porphyry and andesite from the Cheviots,wacke sandstone and wacke siltstone from the Southern Uplands.The till is commonly weathered reddish brown and decalcified at the top, locally with vertical prismatic jointing.The till typically includes ribbon-shaped lenses (palaeochannels) of gravel, fine- to medium-grained, cross-laminated sand and laminated silt and clay, typically in fining-upwards sequences that have been increasingly subglacially sheared, folded and glacitectonized upwards.The lenses typically are up to 1.5m thick (locally <12m), and have planar horizontal contacts with overlying till units. Up to seven cyclic sequences have been recorded."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Generally a sharp uneven contact on bedrock, but locally a complex gradational glaciotectonic contact with fractured or decomposed bedrock. Locally a sharp, uneven to planar horizontal contact with orange brown sand and gravel of the Maiden's Hall Sand and Gravel Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.16e-01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 25m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Ground surface or unconformable contacts with younger superficial deposits.Towards the coast the formation is capped by very stiff,dark brown or purplish brown weathering to reddish brown,stony silty sandy clay diamicton containing clasts similar to the Acklinton Till, but including shell fragments (Horden Till Formation).The contact is either gradational or a sharp, planar, unconformable contact."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.18e-02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Acklinton Formation Of Bowen (1999)"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:NPEG ;
    skos:definition "Clay with subsidiary sand and trace gravel. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Devensian Stage (Quaternary Period) (.116-.0118 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ANTI"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Acklinton Till Formation"@en ;
.

