namedrockunit:BLAW
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CZ"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DMTN"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Killearn Borehole (Browne and McMillan, 1989, Plate 1, Figure 8) is the standard section for the Blane Water Silt Formation (0.5-15.61m depth). The typical lithology is of regular interbedded clay and silt forming varves with dark coloured tops. The clays are silty, reddish brown and brownish grey, with grey silt, some sand layers and wisps, and thin beds of diamicton up to 7cm thick. The clay is firm to stiff and of medium to high plasticity. Isolated stones up to 2cm are interpreted as dropstones. Lithological associations of the formation are consistent with deposition of muds on a lake bottom in contact and near to glacier ice."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Blane Water Silt Formation rests with angular unconformity on older Quaternary sediments including on the Gartocharn Till Formation (GATI) inside the Lock Lomond Stadial ice limit and outside on the Wilderness Till Formation of the Caledonia Glacigenic Group (Midland Valley Glacigenic Subgroup). In Strathblane the formation rests on members of the Clyde Clay Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H3855 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.16e-01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Veneer to over 15m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Blane Water Silt Formation is overlain conformably and by glaciotectonically emplaced younger sediments of the Drumbeg Sand and Gravel Formation and Gartocharn Till Formation in the Endrick valley outside and at the Loch Lomond Stadial ice limit. In Strathblane it is exposed at the surface."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.18e-02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Blane Water Formation [Obsolete: use BLAW]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CGDR ;
    skos:definition "Clay, silt and silty clay with subsidiary sand and trace diamicton. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Devensian Stage (Quaternary Period) (.116-.0118 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "BLAW"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Blane Water Silt Formation"@en ;
.

