namedrockunit:CRR
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/A> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CALSIR"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MSDR"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PEL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PSAMG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PSAMM"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PSMQZ"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/QZITE"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SEMPEL"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Interlayered psammite, semipelite and pelite, with semipelite dominant. Contains beds of calc-silicate rock, especially in the vicinity of Towie (NJ 440 127)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Not seen: a major antiform, now largely obscured by the Tomnaverie, Coull and Lumphanan intrusions, causes the formation to be flanked by younger rocks both to the north and to the south."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H168 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1e+03 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Several kilometres, but not measurable due to the intense folding and migmatisation over most of the outcrop."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Change to the more uniform semipelitic lithology, but with mappable units of gritty psammite, which characterises the Suie Hill Formation. Upper contact to the west of the Rhynie Devonian inlier occurs at the base of a magnetic pelite unit (NJ 394 246 to NJ 459 215). To the east of the Rhynie Devonian it runs through poorly exposed ground (NJ 471 165 to NJ 622 184), where it abuts the Bennachie Granite. The fault-displaced contact reappears at NJ 630 134 to NJ 650 134."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 5.41e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Donside Gneisses [Obsolete: use CRR]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:DBTV ;
    skos:definition "Semipelite with subsidiary calcsilicate-rock, pelite and psammite and trace conglomerate, gritty psammite, metasedimentary rock based on grain size or crystal size, quartzite and quartzose psammite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Neoproterozoic Era (Precambrian) (1000-541 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "CRR"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Craigievar Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:CRRC
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/TUF"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Central and Northern North Sea: The Carrack Formation comprises essentially non-calcareous, carbonaceous, pyritic, micaceous mudstones and siltstones with local sandstones. Mudstones are medium to dark grey and black (locally red-brown: e.g. Well 14/4-1), firm to hard and blocky to fissile. They have a low average sonic velocity. Thin greenish white tuffaceous layers correspond to high gamma-ray spikes (e.g. Well 15/21a-7 at 3142 m) and broader peaks (e.g. Well 14/20-8 at c. 2698 m) on wire line logs. Thin beds of white to buff, microcrystalline, argillaceous limestones, dolomitic limestones and chalky mudstones are interbedded with the mudstones and produce relatively high velocity spikes (e.g. Well 14/19-13). Local sandstones are fine- to coarse-grained and each sandstone unit usually displays upward fining. One of these sandstone units has been given member status (Skiff Sandstone Member). \r
\r
Southern North Sea: The fomation comprises poorly calcareous, occasionally sandy, dark-grey to red-brown and variagated mudstones. Sonic velocity is low. Thin sandy beds and phosphatic pebbles produce occasional high gamma peaks."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """Central and Northern North Sea: The base is normally taken at the downward change from dark grey-black, non-calcareous and carbonaceous mudstone (Carrack Formation) to chestnut brown, brick red, reddish grey or pale calcareous mudstones and chalky mudstones (Valhall Formation). It is marked on wireline logs by a sharp downward increase in velocity and density. Locally in the Central and Northern North Sea the formation rests on the Wick Sandstone and Britannia Sandstone formations. \r
\r
Southern North Sea: There is a sharp downwards change  from dark, poorly calcareous mudstones to paler, harder, chalky mudstone (Valhall Formation) and a corresponding downward increase in velocity and decrease in gamma values."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KP> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.214e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 100 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the formation is normally marked by a downward change from pale to dark grey and red brown calcareous mudstones, chalky mudstones and thin, interbedded limestones (Rødby Formation) to dark grey and black, non-calcareous (or poorly calcareous in the Southern North Sea) mudstones. It is marked by a downward increase in gamma-ray values and a downward decrease in velocity and density. Where the Rødby Formation is absent, the Chalk Group rests unconformably on the Carrack Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.005e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CRKN ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and siltstone with subsidiary limestone, sandstone and tuff. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Aptian Age - to - Albian Age (Cretaceous Period) (121.4-100.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
