namedrockunit:DNDGR
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """[Obsolete: use LEY] \r
\r
Gravel with subsidiary beds of sand. Dominantly pale olive brown and pale pinkish brown pebble-cobble-boulder gravels. Typically clast supported with medium to coarse quartzose and felsitic sand matrix. Clasts are typically subrounded and rounded and dominated by felsite with less common metasediments, red granites and mafic igneous lithologies. Sand interbeds are medium to coarse grained and both felsitic and quartzose. Locally the gravels and sands display Fe and Mn stains and cements. The deposit displays large-scale cross-beds and is locally deformed (faulted and folded by ice melt-out). Palaeocurrent analysis indicates transport to the northwest, west and southwest. Interpreted as glaciodeltaic and glaciofluvial outwash."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the unit has been observed in geological test pits within Leys Quarry (disused) to have a sharp, planar, erosional contact on olive-brown sandy diamicton of the Leys Till Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H3365 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QA> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.8e-01 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "3 - 6m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "In the northwest and northeast faces of Leys Quarry (disused) the formation is observed to have an erosional upper boundary. The overlying Pitscow Sand and Gravel Formation was deposited into a -2m deep channel eroded into the Denend Gravel Formation. In other (southern) faces of the quarry the upper boundary is the ground surface. In this situation the gravels typically display more recent periglacial disturbance (head deposits, involutions and ice-wedge casts)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QO> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.26e-01 ;
    lexicon:isObsoleteEquivalentOf namedrockunit:LEY ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/deprecated> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Denend Member [Obsolete: use DNDGR]"@en ,
        "Leys Gravel Formation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Obsolete name. Gravel with subsidiary sand. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Anglian Stage - to - Wolstonian Stage (Quaternary Period) (.48-.126 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "DNDGR"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Denend Gravel Formation [Obsolete: use LEY]"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:DNLA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/AGG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/BA"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LPTUF"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Drumnessie Lava Member consists of several lavas with intercalations, which make up to 50% of the total thickness of the member, of agglomerate and variably stratified lapilli-tuff, composed substantially of accidental and 'accessory' pyroclasts. The agglomerates include breadcrust bombs. The lavas are mostly plagioclase-, olivine-plagioclase-, and olivine-microphyric basalts (of 'Jedburgh' transitional to  'Dalmeny' types), but there is also one clinopyroxene-olivine-macrophyric basalt ('ankaramite'  of 'Craiglockhart' type). The lavas exhibit proximal characteristics and several nearby necks of the South Campsie Linear Vent-swarm are plugged by basalt of similar type (e.g. near St Mirren's well [NS721 795]), and are probable sources. The BGS Tak-ma-doon borehole intersected the middle and basal lavas of this member."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base is unconformable. In the BGS Tak-ma-doon borehole the basal lava of the Drumnessie Lava Member was seen to overlie mudstone, siltstone and nodular dolomitic limestone of the Ballagan Formation. However, 2 km to the west, in the Bachille Burn [NS 707 793], the member rests directly on sandstone and nodular limestone of the Kinnesswood Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H215 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CJ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.445e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 60 m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Drumnessie Lava Member is overlain conformably or disconformably by the Laird's Loup Lava Member. The lithological change is to plagioclase-macrophyric basalt lava (of 'Markle' type)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CR> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.309e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Drumnessie Lavas [Obsolete: use DNLA]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CPV ;
    skos:definition "Basalt with subsidiary agglomerate and lapilli-tuff. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Arundian Substage - to - Asbian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (344.5-330.9 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "DNLA"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Drumnessie Lava Member"@en ;
.

