namedrockunit:KHLA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/BA"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/HAW"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Kilsyth Hills Lava Member consists predominantly of plagioclase-macrophyric basalts and trachybasalts (hawaiites of 'Markle' type). The lower of these lavas tend to have sparse and small plagioclase phenocrysts and are transitional to plagiclase-microphyric basalts and trachybasalts (hawaiities of 'Jedburgh' type). Some higher lavas in the sequence are olivine-augite-plagioclase-macrophyric basalts (of 'Dunsapie' type). Also, there is a local intercalation of variably olivine-plagioclase-microphyric basalt lava (of 'Jedburgh' to 'Dalmeny' type). The member is equivalent laterally to the Denny Muir Lava Member which is recognised to the east of the Tak-ma-doon Fault. The lavas were thought by Craig (1980) to have been derived from the Waterhead Centre ('Waterhead Central Volcanic Complex')."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Over most of its outcrop, the base is conformable or disconformable on basaltic-trachyandesite (mugearite) and/or trachybasalt (hawaiite) of the Upper Lecket HIll Lava Member, but towards the south-east, the member rests unconformably on progressively older members, including the Lower Lecket Hill Lava Member, the lower south Campsie lavas (Campsie Lava Member) and  the Tappetknowe Lava Member."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H993 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CJ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.445e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "More than 160 m, top eroded."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Kilsyth Hills Lava Member occupies summit areas across its outcrop and the top of the member has been eroded."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CR> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.309e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
