        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SPELGN"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Schistose psammite and semipelite. Locally gneissose semipelite in north."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Top of the highest metacarbonate rock unit of the Loch Tay Limestone Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H1424 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1e+03 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Not known because of folding."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Base of thick green-bed unit that separates the formation from the overlying Cairn Gibbs Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 5.41e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Southern Highland Group (Undivided) [Obsolete: use CGPS, MBPS]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:SOHI ;
    skos:definition "Psammite and semipelite and trace calcareous psammite, calcareous semipelite, gneissose semipelite, gritty psammite, metalava, metatuff and quartzite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Neoproterozoic Era (Precambrian) (1000-541 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "MBPS"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mount Blair Psammite And Semipelite Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:MBSA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/K> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/FGSST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/GLSST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDSTGR"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Osborne White (1921) described the formation as \"highly ferruginous coarse sand or grit\" and Casey (1961) described it as \"gritty, reddish-brown sands with pebbles and phosphatic nodules\". There is a concentration of nodules at the base. The nodules contain abundant shelly material including ammonites, gastropods and bivalves."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the formation is a sharp eroded surface where there is a marked down-section change from gritty, reddish-brown sandstone to the yellow and white quartz sandstone at the top of the underlying rhythmical succession of the Sandrock Formation. This depositional break is called the 'Mid tardefurcata break' by Casey (1961)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:CAW ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.132e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 21.9m at Red Cliff. Thickens towards the north-east from as little as 5cm at Punfield on the Dorset coast, and on the Isle of Wight, to 1.8m at Compton Bay, 3.7m at Blackgang, 9.1m at Bonchurch (Osborne White, 1921)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is gradational, where the reddish brown ferruginous coarse-grained sandstones of the Monk's Bay Sandstone Formation pass up into dark grey mudstones of the Gault Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KA> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.005e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Carstone (Isle of Wight) [Obsolete: use MBSA]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:LGS ;
    skos:definition "Ferruginous sandstone and gritty sandstone with subsidiary glauconitic sandstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Albian Age (Cretaceous Period) (113.2-100.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "MBSA"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monk's Bay Sandstone Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:MBY
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Coarse- to very fine-grained green-grey sandstones (68.2%), interlaminated/thinly interbedded medium- to very fine-grained sandstones and red-brown siltstones (14.9%), intraformational conglomerates (9.7%) and red-brown siltstones (7.3%). All three sandstone categories in the Cosheston Group - litharenite, sublitharenite and subarkose - are present, but the last is restricted to this Formation. c.30% of the Formation is affected by synsedimentary deformation structures."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the Formation is conformable on the underlying Llanstadwell Formation. It is defined at Burton Cliff northeast of Burton Hawn [SM 98967 05258], at the base of an 11.8m-thick multistorey complex of green-grey, interbedded intraformational conglomerates, horizontally or low-angle parallel-laminated F/M to VF sandstones and a few thin siltstones, (see Thomas et al, 2006, figures 9 and 10). The base (at 110.34m above the base of the section measured by Thomas et al, 2006) is a low relief (?0.2m) scour surface overlain by a 0.05m-thick intraformational conglomerate."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H4164 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DO> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.19e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "540 to 600m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is a conformable, transitional one with the overlying Lawrenny Cliff Formation. The 139.91m-thick section at Mill Bay (east) starts a few metres below the high water mark on the northeast side of Mill Bay inlet, at the base of a 1.59m-thick, fine-grained, horizontally and low-angle parallel-laminated sandstone [SN 0026 0491], and ends in the Lawrenny Cliff Formation, this being the only section that exposes the boundary (see Thomas et al, 2006, Figure 14B), which intersects the high water mark 60m southwest of the quay [SN 00570 05260]."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DP> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.105e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Lower Cosheston Division [Obsolete: use LLSW, MBY]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:COB ;
    skos:definition "Sandstone with subsidiary siltstone and trace conglomerate. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Lochkovian Age - to - Pragian Age (Devonian Period) (419-410.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "MBY"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mill Bay Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:MCG
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CALSST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SALMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Sandstone, fine-grained, bedded, clayey in lower part passing calcareous and shelly upwards with scattered ooids, generally poorly fossiliferous; locally a sandy ooidal limestone with trigoniid bivalves, gastropods and ammonites is developed at the top (Newbridge Trigonia Bed; Coe, 1995, p. 169)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Conformable: sharp change upwards into calcareous fine-grained sandstone from sandy ooidal limestone (Hambleton Oolite Member). Where this is absent, difficult to distinguish from Birdsall Calcareous Grit Member."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H4183 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.615e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "12 to 15m in the Tabular Hills thinning to zero south of Malton and near Scarborough, less than 2m at Spiker's Hill Quarry."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Conformable or non-sequence: sharp change up from calcareous fine-grained sandstone or locally a sandy ooidal limestone (Newbridge Trigonia Bed) into generally non-sandy ooidal limestone (Malton Oolite Member), at the base of a black ooid-limestone bed at Newbridge Quarry (Coe, 1995)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.548e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Middle Calcareous Grit [Obsolete: use MCG]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:COO ;
    skos:definition "Calcareous sandstone and sandstone and trace sandy limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Oxfordian Age (Jurassic Period) (161.5-154.8 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "MCG"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Middle Calcareous Grit Member"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:MCH
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/O> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Well-bedded sandstone with mudstone interbeds. A thin phosphatic conglomerate horizon at the top."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Gradational contact taken at the base of the first prominent sandstone lying above muddy sandstones of the Penrhyn Du Member."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H3236 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/OR> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.771e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/OR> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "<8m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Sharply overlain by dark grey mudstones with sandstone and siltstone laminae of the Llanengan Formation. The boundary is taken at the top of a districtive phosphatic conglomerate bed within the Machroes Member. The contact may be a disconformity or non-sequence."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/OR> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.68e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Passage Beds [Obsolete: use BOOC, SGBK, CLGB, MCH, UND, YED, MYS]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:STUD ;
    skos:definition "Sandstone with subsidiary mudstone and trace conglomerate. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Arenig Series (Ordovician Period) (477.1-468 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "MCH"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Machroes Member"@en ;
.

