namedrockunit:NGRC
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CAMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Limestone, grey, thinly-bedded, muddy, interbedded in the west with thinly-bedded variably calcareous mudstone, in the east with flaggy calcareous sandstone. Generally poorly fossiliferous, although ammonites are known, and sponge spicules are common in the more calcareous beds."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "In the Hambleton Hills, base a non-sequence, with interbedded muddy limestone and calcareous mudstone resting sharply on the eroded top of the rubbly coralliferous shelly limestone of the Coral Rag Member (Coralline Oolite Formation). In the type area in the Howardian Hills, interbedded muddy limestone and calcareous sandstone overlies grey and brown mudstone of the Langton Clay Member. In the Brown Moor Borehole (SE86SW/4 [81266 62043] ), calcareous siltstone rests sharply on the eroded top of fine-grained limestone inferred to represent the Malton Oolite Member (Coralline Oolite Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H3578 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.615e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "9 to 13m in the Hambleton Hills, 16 to 22m in the Howardian Hills, 22.1m in the Brown Moor Borehole (SE86SW/4 [81266 62043]) (Gaunt et al., 1980), possibly including the underlying Langton Clay Member."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "In the Hambleton Hills, sharp change up from interbedded muddy limestone and calcareous mudstone into fine-grained sandstone, calcareous, spicule-rich, bioturbated, massive, ammonitiferous (Spaunton Sandstone Member). In the Howardian Hills and at Brown Moor Borehole, sharp change up into grey mudstone of Ampthill Clay Formation, probably a non-sequence."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.548e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Cement-Stone [Obsolete: use NGRC]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:UCG ;
    skos:definition "Calcareous mudstone and limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Oxfordian Age (Jurassic Period) (161.5-154.8 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "NGRC"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "North Grimston Cementstone Member"@en ;
.

