        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Gravelly sandy clay diamicton, compact and massive, matrix-supported, generally reddish brown. The diamicton contains well dispersed angular to well rounded clasts of red sandstone, Borrowdale Volcanic Group rocks, Skiddaw Group slates, limestone and yellow and white Coal Measure sandstones, coal, granophyre, granodiorite (?Criffel) and greywacke sandstone. The member also contains \"rip-up\" clasts of underlying deposits and its composition can vary considerably locally (being largely determined by the nature of the underlying sequences from which it is mainly derived)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Unconformable on older-Quaternary deposits and its base is commonly repeated by small-scale thrusting. At its type section and other sites within the buried valleys of the Ehen, Calder and Lower Uldale, the Rothersyke Till Member overlies the Ehen Valley Sand and Gravel Member of the Seascale Glacigenic Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H4662 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.16e-01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Typically 1.0 to 2.0m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Commonly the ground surface, but at its Type Section the Member is overlain by the Low Mill Gravel Member of the Gosforth Glacigenic Formation, and at other localities, by the Peel Place Sand and Gravel Member of the Gosforth Glacigenic Formation or by Holocene deposits such as peat and alluvium. At its Reference Section the upper surface of the till (the ground surface) displays a polygonal pattern of thermal contraction cracks (relict tundra patterned ground). In the coastal area north of Sellafield, the gravelly reddish brown Rothersyke Till Member is overlain (or is cut out by) the sandy and clayey How Man Till Member of the Gosforth Glacigenic Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.18e-02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Upper Boulder Clay [Obsolete: use FWTI, GOGL, HMTI, PKSA, PLBT, RSTI, STPTG]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:GOGL ;
    skos:definition "Diamicton with subsidiary clay, gravel, sand and silt. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Devensian Stage (Quaternary Period) (.116-.0118 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "RSTI"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rothersyke Till Member"@en ;
.

