namedrockunit:STIE
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/D> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DOLO"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Struie Formation is largely composed of grey or reddish brown, micaceous siltstone and mudstone, with only occasional thin beds of very fine-grained, greyish brown, silty, calcareous sandstone (Richards, 1990). These sediments include highly radioactive horizons that are likely to be organic-rich partings. They also include occasional laminations of black mudstone, microcrystalline dolomite, and micritic limestone. The siltstones are commonly rippled, and Richards (1985) identified small slump structures in core from 12/27-1. The siltstones and mudstones overlie a basal Devonian quartzite at 12/29-2, comprising grey and pink pseudomorphic quartz grains cemented by calcite and dolomite. Beds of conglomerate are interbedded within a 68 m siltstone section in the middle of the Struie Formation in well 12/28-2; the conglomerate comprises pebbles of igneous and metamorphic basement in a very fine to very coarse grained sandy matrix."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Struie Formation rests on metamorphic or granitic basement in all sections."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:STRU ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DL> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.19e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DL> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "A complete, 521 m-thick Lower Devonian section was encountered in well 12/29-2, whereas the sediments are at least 976 m thick in nearby well 12/27-1. Equivalent sediments are 2400 m thick along strike in Ross and Cromarty (Armstrong, 1977)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Lower Old Red Sandstone is an angular unconformity throughout Scotland (Mykura, 1991), except at the centre of the Orcadian Basin, where there is a conformable transition to Middle Old Red Sandstone. In southern Quadrant 12, the Struie Formation is overlain by Middle Devonian alluvial-fan sandstones and conglomerates or by Permian sediments. In well 13/19-1, lacustrine Lower Devonian sediments are overlain by fluvial Middle Devonian deposits, and the boundary is defined by a sharp downhole increase in gamma-ray response, velocity and resistivity, and by a change in dipmeter response."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DL> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.943e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Struie Group [Obsolete: use STIE]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:LOOR ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and siltstone with subsidiary conglomerate and sandstone and trace dolostone and limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Early Devonian Epoch (Devonian Period) (419-394.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "STIE"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Struie Formation"@en ;
.

