namedrockunit:UPSCL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CH"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CS"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Upper Shelly Clay mainly comprises brown and dark grey to black shelly clay, sandy clay and muddy limestone with fossil oysters. It appears to become more sandy towards the south-east and east (Ellison et al., 2004, fig. 19) although King (in prep.) suggests that these occurrences may be partly incised channel-filling units that post-date the Upper Shelly Clay. \r
\r
The Upper Shelly Clay includes a distinctive bed of grey argillaceous shelly limestone with a conchoidal fracture, typically about 0.2 m thick but locally up to 1 m (Ellison, 1983), containing the fossil shells of freshwater molluscs. This is known as the 'Paludina Limestone', the Paludina Bed (or Band) (Curry, 1958; Rickman, 1861) or the Woolwich Freshwater Bed (Dewey and Bromehead, 1921). \r
\r
The Upper Shelly Clay was deposited in marginal marine lagoons with freshwater incursions. \r
\r
Earliest Eocene (Ypresian)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Upper Shelly Clay rests on a burrowed erosive surface overlying either the Reading Formation or the Laminated Beds or the Lower Shelly Clay of the Woolwich Formation. The contact with the Laminated Beds may be a rapid gradation. The 'Paludina Limestone' acts as a marker horizon, which has been used to show that the Upper Shelly Clay rests directly on the Lower Shelly Clay in south-east London in the vicinity of Petts Wood and St Mary Cray [TQ 45 68] (Ellison et al., 2004, p. 29 and fig. 14; Whitaker, 1872, p. 116)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H5087 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 5.6e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/U> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 5 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Upper Shelly Clay underlies the Harwich Formation (Thames Group) at a channelled erosion surface. This marks a change in lithology from shelly clay, sandy clay and muddy limestone of the Upper Shelly Clay to glauconite-bearing sands, silts or clays with a basal rounded flint gravel bed and a marine fauna, or sandy flint gravels of the Harwich Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.81e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Woolwich Shell Bed(s) [Obsolete: use LSCL, UPSCL]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:WL ;
    skos:definition "Limestone, sandy clay and shelly clay. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Ypresian Age (Palaeogene Period) (56-48.1 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "UPSCL"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Upper Shelly Clay (Woolwich Formation)"@en ;
.

