        "Alluvial fan deposits"@en ,
        "Alluvium"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:FLUV ;
    skos:definition "Clay, gravel, sand and silt and trace peat. Superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Quaternary Period (2.58-0 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ALAF"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alluvium and alluvial fan deposits (undifferentiated)"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:ALNS
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SPICUT"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The member comprises sandy spiculite grading to spiculitic sandstone. Ovoidal spicules dominate, being derived from the siliceous sponge Rhaxella. Spicules are very fine- to fine-grained sand grade. The sandstone is generally intensely bioturbated, but in the type section, wavy, horizontal laminae are preserved at the base.  Bivalves, belemnites and ammonites occur in the type section.  Two large scale upward coarsening cycles occurs from grey-brown, spiculitic siltstone into sandy spiculites.  An upward fining unit grading from sandy spiculite to spiculitic siltstone is commonly present at the top of the member. \r
\r
A thin mudstone unit, or where this is missing, a higher gamma argillaceous horizon can be used to subdivide the member (Stephen et al., 1993)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base is defined by a down-section transitional change from spiculites to mudstones. The boundary is marked by an inflection on the gamma-ray and sonic logs."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H5138 ,
        namedrockunit:H5139 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.615e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 90 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the member is defined by an abrupt downward change from mudstones to spiculites in some sections, but a more transitional change in others. The boundary is marked by a decrease in gamma-ray values and a decrease in velocity."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.548e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Alness Member [Obsolete: use ALNS]"@en ,
        "Alness Spiculite Formation [Obsolete: use ALNS]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:HTHE ;
    skos:definition "Spiculite with subsidiary mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Oxfordian Age (Jurassic Period) (161.5-154.8 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ALNS"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alness Spiculite Member"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:AOL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMOOL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Limestone, white to pale yellow, poorly fossiliferous ooid-grainstones and ooidal limestones, in the south-west they are generally well sorted medium-grained and massive, rarely cross-bedded; in the north-east they become more bioclastic, less well sorted fine- to coarse-grained, commonly cross-bedded. Generally capped by bored, encrusted hardground (mapped on sheets 234 (1972 edition) and 251 (1970 edition) as "chinastone" or "Coppice Limestone" (Cave, 1977)). Lateral passage north-east into peloidal packstones and wackestones of the Ardley Member (White Limestone Formation), and the lower part passes south-westwards by decrease in ooid content into the Tresham Rock Formation. At Minchinhampton Common, about 0.5m of very shelly calcareous mudstone and brownish coarse-grained ooidal limestone beds ('The Scroff') occur at the base of the formation (Channon, 1950). \r
\r
High energy shallow carbonate shelf with mobile ooid shoals."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "South of Horsley [ST 840 975]; rapid passage up from fine-grained slightly argillaceous limestone with ooidal limestone lenses (Tresham Rock Formation), into massive ooid-limestones. Locally a thin grey mudstone bed may intervene. North of Horsley; sharp change up from grey mainly calcareous mudstone (Fuller's Earth Formation), or where absent, from shell-detrital limestone of the Dodington Ash Rock Member (Fuller's Earth Formation), into massive ooid-limestones. At Minchinhampton Common [SO 85 01] very shelly calcareous mudstone (part of 'The Scroff') at the base of the formation rests sharply on ooid limestone of the Minchinhampton Limestone Member (Taynton Limestone Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H645 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.682e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "About 3 to 21 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Bored and oyster encrusted hardground on ooidal limestone, overlain by pale grey, bioclastic ooidal limestones of Chalfield Oolite Formation, or where absent, brown and grey variably ooidal, sandy or muddy bioclastic lime-grainstone/packstone (Forest Marble Formation). Near the formation's southern limit, grey mudstone of the Fuller's Earth Formation intervenes beneath the Chalfield Oolite."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.653e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Bath Stone and Lower Rags [Obsolete: use AOL]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:GOG ;
    skos:definition "Ooid-limestone with subsidiary limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs both onshore and offshore. Deposited during the Bathonian Age (Jurassic Period) (168.2-165.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower namedrockunit:CWCL ;
    skos:notation "AOL"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Athelstan Oolite Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:AOPT
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
