        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SIMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Grey, calcareous, bioturbated silicate mudstones, locally very finely sandy and micaceous, which generally increases upwards. In upper part, beds up to 0.5m thick of pale grey calcareous very fine-grained silicate-sandstone or finely sandy limestone occur. Fauna includes ammonites."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Base probably a non-sequence: sandy mudstone resting sharply on fossiliferous fine limestones of the Beacon Limestone Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1488 ,
        namedrockunit:H204 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.842e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JT> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Dorset coast: 10 to 21m. Inland Dorset: 0 to 121m (Bristow et al., 1995). East Somerset up to 12m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Top is marked by the change up from sandy mudstone to sands or sandstones of the undivided Bridport Sand Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JT> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.747e+02 ;
