        "Lower Limestone Formation"@en ,
        "Lower Limestone Group (Undivided) [Obsolete: use ENT]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CKN ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and sandstone with subsidiary seat-earth sandstone and siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Brigantian Substage - to - Pendleian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (330.9-327 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ENT"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enterkin Mudstone Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:ERYG
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DMTN"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Moderately stiff, dark grey to blue-grey, generally massive, pebbly, silty clay, characteristically containing Snowdonian Ordovician volcanic clasts but dominated by local Lower Palaeozoic clasts. Colour and dominant clast composition variable and generally reflect the local bedrock geology. Also includes other glacigenic deposits of similar composition, notably glaciofluvial sand and gravel and morainic deposits."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Generally unconformable on bedrock, but locally may overlie Devensian head deposits (McCarroll, 2005, p.29); Lleyn Till Member of St Asaph Glacigenic Formation (Lleyn Formation of Young et al., 2002), or post-Ipswichian pre-glacial peats (Addison and Edge, 1992)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1529 ,
        namedrockunit:H3390 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QDD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.6e-02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QDD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Highly variable, locally likely to greatly exceed 10 m (Warren et al., 1984)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Generally the ground surface or unconformable contact with overlying alluvial deposits, peat and head (Britannia Catchemnts Group). May locally be unconformably overlain by red diamicton of the Lleyn Formation (of Harris et al., 1997), Lleyn Till Member, St Asaph Glacigenic Formation (McMillan et al., 2011)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QDD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.47e-02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "North Welsh Boulder Clay [Obsolete: use ERYG]"@en ,
        "Welsh Till [Obsolete: use ERYG]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:WALES ;
    skos:definition "Diamicton with subsidiary gravel and sand and trace clay and silt. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Dimlington Stadial (Quaternary Period) (.026-.0147 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ERYG"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eryry Glacigenic Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:ESA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CLSDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Elgol Sandstone Formation comprises bioturbated, clay-rich sandstone intercalated with silty fissile mudstone, overlain by coarsening-upwards white, pure, non-calcareous sandstone."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Elgol Sandstone Formation overlies the Cullaidh Shale Formation with a gradational boundary. The base of the Elgol Sandstone Formation is marked by the first appearance of relatively intensely bioturbated, pro-deltaic clay-rich sandstones intercalated with the dark fissile mudstones typical of the Cullaidh Shale Formation (Hudson and Harris, 1979, figs 2-4; Harris and Hudson, 1980, fig.5; Harris, 1989, figs 3, 6, 8, 10; Morton and Hudson, 1995, fig.19). The Elgol Sandstone Formation is significantly less fossiliferous than the underlying Cullaidh Shale Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H235 ,
        namedrockunit:H4640 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JB> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.709e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Thicknesses from Harris and Hudson (1980) except where indicated. At the type section in Strathaird, the Elgol Sandstone Formation is 22m thick. Northwards, on the Trotternish Peninsula, its thickness varies between 9m at Rigg, at least 12m at Invertote and 17 to 32m (BGS, 2007) in south Trotternish and Raasay. Farther south, the formation is discontinuous with thin (<1m) brown silty fissile shales present on Eigg, although Emeleus (1997) disputes its presence."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The boundary of the Elgol Sandstone Formation with the overlying Lealt Shale Formation is sharply-defined.  The uppermost bed of the Elgol Sandstone is a very coarse sandstone with local developments of granules and pebbles, and this is abruptly overlain by the silty or bituminous fissile mudstones of the Lealt Shale (Harris and Hudson, 1980, fig.5). The formation is unconformably overlain by extrusive rocks of the Paleocene Skye Lava Group in places."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.653e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Great White Sandstone [Obsolete: use ESA]"@en ,
        "White Sandstone [Obsolete: use LAWS, ESA]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:GEST ;
    skos:definition "Sandstone with subsidiary clayey sandstone and silty mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs both onshore and offshore. Deposited during the Bajocian Age - to - Bathonian Age (Jurassic Period) (170.9-165.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "ESA"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Elgol Sandstone Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:ESH
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/C> ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent namedrockunit:BSG ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/FEST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
