        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Calcareous glauconitic sand and glauconitic sandy silty chalk with phosphatic nodules."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The lower boundary is taken at the base of glauconite-rich sand or clay resting disconformably, at a strongly burrowed surface, on pale grey siltstones of the Upper Greensand (or grey mudstones of the Gault), which contain only a trace of glauconite at the most. Note: bioturbation may carry glauconitic sediment down into the underlying formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H2828 ,
        namedrockunit:H4494 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KE> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.005e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KE> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "2 to 4 m throughout much of the Southern Province, might be only a few tenths of a metre elsewhere over synsedimentary structural highs. Up to 5 m in Kent and at Eastbourne, East Sussex."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is taken at the upwards cessation of sand and/or abundant glauconitic grains (and phosphatic pebbles) (can occur as a rapid transition over 10 cm or less) into grey, sparsely glauconitic chalky marl or marly chalk, comprising the rest of the West Melbury Marly Chalk Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KE> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 9.39e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Basement Bed [Obsolete: use GLML]"@en ,
        "Chloritic Marl [Obsolete: use BRHD, CBG, GLML]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:WMCH ;
    skos:definition "Calcareous mudstone, sand and siltstone with subsidiary glauconitic sandstone and sandstone and trace limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Cenomanian Age (Cretaceous Period) (100.5-93.9 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "GLML"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Glauconitic Marl Member"@en ;
