namedrockunit:KYLE
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/ANHY"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Kyle Group is characterized by white, buff, brownish grey, or pink limestones and thin grey shales, with an overlying, more variable unit of marine, lagoonal, and basin-marginal sabkha facies. The limestones of the basal unit are generally argillaceous, with a thin basal mudflake conglomerate in the Auk Field. Their texture varies between cryptocrystalline, microcrystalline, and coarsely crystalline. They contain both tabulate and rugose corals in the Argyll Field (Pennington 1975), and have also yielded ostracods, brachiopods, bivalves, gastropods, and crinoids. In well 38/3-1, the interbedded shales contain ostracods and a sparse brachiopod fauna that includes lingulids and strophomenids. \r
\r
In well 38/3-1, the limestones are overlain by dark grey mudstones containing brachiopods and veins of dolomite. In different sections in the Auk-Argyll area, equivalent sediments include microcrystalline dolomite (e.g. 30/25a-2), reddish brown or grey calcareous mudstone with thin limestone and dolomite beds (e.g. 30/16-5), or interbedded sandy siltstone, shale, anhydrite and dolomite (e.g. 30/24-3)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "An angular unconformity separates Kyle Group limestones from underlying reddened Lower Paleozoic turbidites and shales in well 30/16-5. The base of the limestones has not been penetrated elsewhere. However, they probably rest on Lower Paleozoic basement at 38/3-1, as Lower Devonian strata are unknown in the Central North Sea south of the offshore continuation of the Southern Uplands Fault (Gatliff et al., 1994)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DV> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.853e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "177 m thick in well 38/3-1; 130 m thick in the Argyll Field."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "In and around the Auk Field, the Kyle Group underlies Lower Permian sandstones, beneath the regional base-Permian unconformity. In well 38/3-1 and in the Argyll Field, the Kyle Group is conformably overlain by the Upper Old Red Group, the boundary being taken at the transition from marine or lagoonal to continental red-bed facies. At 38/3-1, the boundary is marked by a downward change from sandstones, conglomerates and minor volcanics to marine mudstones, but does not generate a significant wireline-log break."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DR> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.711e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:definition "Anhydrite-stone, limestone and mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Givetian Age - to - Frasnian Age (Devonian Period) (385.3-371.1 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:isTopConceptOf lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:KYLI ,
        namedrockunit:KYMU ;
    skos:notation "KYLE"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kyle Group"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:KYQU
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/A> ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent namedrockunit:DBDQ ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LAVA"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/QZITE"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/VLSS"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Quartzite, massive, gritty and cross-bedded in places. Basal part locally more psammitic. 'Boulder Bed' locally near base (with granite clasts in some outcrops). Amphibolitic pillow lavas and metavolcanic rocks near the base in Kymah Burn."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Lower limit of massive quartzite overlying either Laddder Hills Formation or Nochty Semipelite and Limestone Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H3507 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1e+03 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Generally 500 - 600m. Variable from 15m to 650m. Thin on Upper Donside (Allargue area)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Upper limit of massive quartzite below Culchavie Striped Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 5.41e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Durn Hill Quartzite Formation"@en ,
        "Durnhill Quartzite [Obsolete: use KYQU]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:DBIS ;
    skos:definition "Quartzite with subsidiary conglomerate, lava and volcaniclastic rocks (both pyroclastic and reworked volcanic rocks). Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Neoproterozoic Era (Precambrian) (1000-541 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "KYQU"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kymah Quartzite Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:KYRE
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Kyrre Formation typically comprises a thick, monotonous sequence of mudstones with sporadic limestone stringers. The mudstones are grey, silty, slightly calcareous and occasionally pyritic, glauconitic and micaceous. The limestones are off-white to pale brown, argillaceous, chalky and locally dolomitic."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the Kyrre Formation is normally marked by a downward change from mudstones to argillaceous chalky limestones and interbedded mudstones of the Macbeth Formation. It is marked on wireline logs by a downward decrease in gamma values and an increase in velocity. Locally, the basal sediments of the Kyrre Formation display a progressive downward decrease in gamma values and an increase in velocity (e.g. 3/10b-1), which is similar to the log signatures displayed by the basal Flounder and Mackerel formations. On intrabasinal highs, the Kyrre Formation locally rests unconformably on pre-Cretaceous rocks. In the South Viking Graben, the Kyrre Formation locally rests on the Herring Formation (e.g. 16/3-1)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 9.39e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The Kyrre Formation reaches a thickness of about 1100 m. Seismic data suggest that it thins by onlap over contemporaneous highs, but condensed basal sections may also occur (Johnson et al., 1993)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Kyrre Formation is usually marked by a downward change from pale grey and commonly red-brown calcareous mudstones and argillaceous chalky limestones of the Jorsalfare Formation (unit J1) to darker grey mudstones. It is marked on wireline logs by a downward increase in gamma values and a decrease in velocity."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KC> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 7.22e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:STLN ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and trace limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Turonian Age - to - Campanian Age (Cretaceous Period) (93.9-72.2 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:DABLS ,
        namedrockunit:WHISA ;
    skos:notation "KYRE"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kyrre Formation"@en ;
.

