namedrockunit:PAIS
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Linwood Borehole (Browne and McMillan, 1989a, Figure 17) is the standard section for the Paisley Clay Member in the Clyde valley west of Govan, and Loch Lomond. Stratigraphical relationships and geographical distribution are in ibid (Figures 2c, 3, 4). The lithofacies are: Clay and silt, finely colour banded in shades of greyish brown and grey; sparse marine fauna (26.5-28.14m in depth). Silt with clay bands, grey, well-bedded; sparse marine microfauna (28.14 - 29.20m). Clay with silt layers, very finely colour laminated in shades of reddish brown and grey (29.20 - 30.23m). Silt with some sand and clay bands; grey, well bedded (30.23 - 31.10m). The succession is thicker and subdivided into three east of Govan. In the Bridgeton Borehole (ibid, Figure 18): Clay, silty, with many silt laminae; colour banded in shades of brownish grey and sometimes reddish brown; rarely fossiliferous (6.9 - 16.23m). Clay, silty, with many silt laminae; colour banded in shades of brownish grey and reddish brown; bands of finely colour laminated clay in shades of reddish brown and grey; rarely fossiliferous (16.23 - 21.77m). Silt with clayey silt and clay bands; thinly laminated to thinly bedded; brownish grey, reddish brown and grey; rarely fossiliferous (21.77 - 29.31m)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Paisley Clay Member of the Clyde Clay Formation rests with angular unconformity or disconformity on older Quaternary sediments or bedrock. It normally rests on the Wilderness Till Formation of the Caledonia Glacigenic Group (Midland Valley Glacigenic Subgroup). However, under the deeper part of the Quaternary sedimentary basin adjacent to the River Clyde it is underlain by the Bridgeton Sand Member of the Clyde Clay Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1375 ,
        namedrockunit:H4242 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.16e-01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Veneer to over 25m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Paisley Clay Member is overlain conformably or unconformably by younger Quaternary sediments. Normally the Linwood Clay Member of the Clyde Clay Formation (of the British Coastal Deposits Group) is in conformable contact but the Clydebank Clay Formation and Clyde Valley Formation of the Britannia Catchments Group (Clyde Catchment Subgroup) is unconformable. It is also extensively exposed at surface in the lower Clyde valley."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.18e-02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Clyde Beds [Obsolete: use PRTS, BOCH, LIWD, PAIS, KARN, CLYD]"@en ,
        "Paisley Formation [Obsolete: use PAIS]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CLYD ;
    skos:definition "Clay and silt with subsidiary sand and trace gravel. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Devensian Stage (Quaternary Period) (.116-.0118 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "PAIS"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Paisley Clay Member"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:PANT
