        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """[Obsolete: use PL] \r
\r
Fossiliferous marine limestones, calcareous and commonly glauconitic, sandstones and sands."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Conglomeratic and glauconitic coarse-grained sand, sandstone and limestone (Upper Lydite Bed) resting disconformably on mudstones, siltstones or fine-grained argillaceous sands and/ sandstones of the Kimmeridge Clay Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H948 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JI> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.492e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JI> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 13 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Upwards change from fully marine limestones with large molluscan fossils to lagoonal fine-grained limestones and marls of the Purbeck Formation, the base of which is commonly marked either by laminated, ostracod-bearing 'Pendle' or oyster-bearing marl, or by a sharp nonsequential junction overlain by generally non-calcareous and commonly ferruginous sands and sandstones of the Lower Greensand or Whitchurch Sand Formation, or by calcareous mudstones of the Gault Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JI> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.431e+02 ;
    lexicon:isObsoleteEquivalentOf
