namedrockunit:TOTL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent namedrockunit:S456 ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/P"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The Tottenhill Sand and Gravel Member is a complex sequence of sands and gravels, dominated by flint. They were formerly well exposed in the Tottenhill Gravel Pit, where two units were evident. A lower unit up to 5 m thick consists of medium to coarse, poorly sorted, mostly angular gravels with cross-bedding dipping steeply (20 to 25 degrees) to the southeast, and with frost wedge and cryoturbation structures. The gravels contain numerous pebbles of woody and reedy peat, soft brown clay, varved clay and Jurassic and Lower Cretaceous erratics. The upper unit is about 3.5 m thick and consists of fine- and medium-grained gravels, which are better sorted than the lower gravels, and have a planar cross-bedding dipping westwards at 8 to 15 degrees. The junction of the two units is marked by a well-defined, slightly uneven surface along which the upper gravels truncate cryturbation and bedding structures in the lower gravel. The lower unit has no known outcrop."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The Tottenhill Sand and Gravel Member unconformably overlies silts and clays of the Nar Valley Clay and the Nar Valley Freshwater Beds with a sharp lithological change. Elsewhere it locally overlies chalky till of uncertain affinity (but probably Lowestoft Formation or Sheringham Cliffs Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H4934 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QPM> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 7.8e-01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QPM> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 14 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Tottenhill Sand and Gravel Member is locally overlain by a cryturbated mixture of dull red-brown, sparsely stony, sandy clay and gravelly sand. This might be a till, although Lewis and Rose (1991) have interpreted this as evidence of a period of temperate-climate soil formation, followed by cold climate disturbance."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QPM> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.26e-01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Tottenhill Gravels [Obsolete: use TOTL]"@en ,
        "Tottenhill Member"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:BRLA ;
