        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SHYLST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Comprises soft, dark grey thinly-bedded mudstones (\"shales\") and mudstones with subordinate beds of pale grey siltstone, fine-grained sandstone (locally calcareous where it is known as Tilgate Stone or colloquially \"Hastings Granite\"), shelly limestone, clay ironstone and rare pebble beds. The top metre or so of the Wadhurst Clay often comprises stiff clay stained red by penecontemporaneous weathering. Thin beds of shelly limestone, rich in Neomiodon and Viviparus, are present throughout.  Nodular clay-ironstone occurs particularly in the lower part of the formation, but also near the top.  Thicker beds of siltstone and lenticular calcareous sandstone units are also present. In the Tunbridge Wells district a thicker sand in the lower part of the formation has been named Hog Hill Sand Member (Allen, 1959; Bristow and Bazley, 1972) but this does not appear on BGS maps. Rare Equisetities fossil soils have been described. The base of the formation is commonly a thicker siltstone and in some areas a basal pebble bed, the Top Ashdown Pebble Bed is present."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base is sharp and marked by the top of the Top Ashdown Pebble Bed locally but where this is absent the boundary is transitional, and placed in siltstones between the underlying fine-grained sandstones of the Ashdown Formation and the mudstones of the Wadhurst Clay Formation. Up-section change from sandstones (Ashdown Formation) into nodular clay-ironstone, silty mudstone and siltstone (Wadhurst Clay Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1267 ,
        namedrockunit:H3123 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KV> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.377e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KV> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The formation thickens westward from 30m at Rye to 70m at Cuckfield and 78m at Worth."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is marked by a sharp change from siltstones  of the Wadhurst Clay Formation up into fine-grained silty sandstones of the Tunbridge Wells Sand Formation. The top of the Wadhurst Clay is commonly red-stained at this junction."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KV> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.326e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Wadhurst Clay [Obsolete: use WDC]"@en ,
        "Wadhurst Formation [Obsolete: use WDC]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:W ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone with subsidiary calcareous sandstone, limestone, sandstone and siltstone and trace ferruginous mudstone and shelly limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Valanginian Age (Cretaceous Period) (137.7-132.6 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "WDC"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wadhurst Clay Formation"@en ;
.

