        "Concretionary Limestone Formation [Obsolete: use CNCL]"@en ,
        "Upper Magnesian Limestone (Durham) [Obsolete: use CNCL, ROD]"@en ,
        "Upper Magnesian Limestone [Obsolete: use SEH, ROD, CNCL]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:ROD ;
    skos:definition "Dolostone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs both onshore and offshore. Deposited during the Guadalupian Epoch - to - Lopingian Epoch (Permian Period) (274.4-251.9 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "CNCL"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Concretionary Limestone Member"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:CNLL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMOOL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Thick bedded, cross bedded, pale grey ooid grainstones with scattered beds of medium grey fine-grained skeletal packstone. Compound ooids of pisolitic proportions common. Thin mudstone associated with the palaeokarstic surface at the top of the Cefnyrhendy Oolite Member, present in the northeast of the Vale of Glamorgan."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the formation is gradational and taken at the incoming of a predominantly oolitic grainstone sequence above the skeletal packstones or skeletal/oolitic packstone/grainstones of the underlying High Tor Limestone Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:SMO ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CJ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.445e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "135m in the west of the Vale of Glamorgan, thickening to 180m in the eastern part of the Vale of Glamorgan."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the formation is taken at the base of the first bed of calcite mudstone of the overlying Stormy Limestone Formation occuring above the ooid grainstones and subordinate skeletal packstones of the Cornelly Oolite Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CQ> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.37e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Seminula Oolite [Obsolete: use CNLL]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:HBO ;
    skos:definition "Ooid-limestone with subsidiary limestone and trace mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Arundian Substage - to - Holkerian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (344.5-337 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:ARL ,
        namedrockunit:CEO ;
    skos:notation "CNLL"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cornelly Oolite Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:CODO
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMOOL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Calcareous ooid-grainstone, white, grey and yellow, fine- to coarse-grained, generally moderately bioclastic, in medium to thick beds, and cross-bedded, with interbedded calcareous mudstone beds in the lower part. Subordinate, impersistent bioclastic limestone beds. Coralliferous, clayey and burrowed limestone locally developed at at least one level, but otherwise whole fossils are uncommon. The basal beds from Tormarton north to Hawkesbury Upton comprise a unit of coarse-grained shelly ooidal limestone (\"Grickstone Beds\"). Wyatt and Cave (2002) recognise lower and upper units of very similar gross lithology separated by a bored hardground."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "South of Badminton [ST 78 83] the base is a sharp upward change from grey calcareous silicate mudstone with subordinate argillaceous limestone beds of the Fuller's Earth Formation into ooid-limestone, locally with limestone pebbles, or coarse-grained shelly ooidal limestone (\"Grickstone Beds\"), locally with limestone pebbles. North of Badminton, the base rests sharply on fine-grained ooid-limestone of the Athelstan Oolite Formation, which is commonly capped by a hardground (separated on sheet 251 (1970 edition) and known as \"chinastone\" or \"Coppice Limestone\" (Cave, 1977)), or locally where that formation is absent, on the underlying fine-grained slightly argillaceous limestone of the Tresham Rock Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H5066 ,
        namedrockunit:H5118 ,
        namedrockunit:H5119 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.682e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "8 to 18m through the Bath to Chippenham area, thinning northwards to zero near Didmarton, and passing into mudstone southwards. 7.83m in Chalfield No.1 Borehole."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Disconformable: moderately bioclastic ooid-limestone of Combe Down Oolite Member, surmounted by planar, locally irregular, bored and oyster-encrusted surface, overlain by generally non-ooidal, pisoidal and shelly limestone of Twinhoe Member. Where latter absent north of Corsham, Combe Down Oolite may be difficult to distinguish from the Bath Oolite Member, which is more sparsely bioclastic. In places in the north of the member's range, its top may be a non-sequence, taken above the highest pale coloured generally moderately bioclastic ooid-grainstone, either erosive or marked by a bored and/or oyster-encrusted hardground, overlain by grey or brown variably sandy bioclastic limestone, variously ooidal or shelly, or locally by calcareous silicate-mudstone (all of the Forest Marble Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.653e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Combe Down Oolite [Obsolete: use CODO]"@en ,
        "Lower Rags (lower part) [Obsolete: use CODO]"@en ,
        "Lower Ragstones (lower part) [Obsolete: use CODO]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:CFDO ;
    skos:definition "Ooid-limestone with subsidiary limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Bathonian Age (Jurassic Period) (168.2-165.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "CODO"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Combe Down Oolite Member"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:COFG
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/D> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/GN"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MCGNA"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The pluton is compositionally zoned comprising a marginal coarse-grained feldspar-phyric granite enclosing a central fine- to medium-grained aphyric granite. The outer zone has been divided into a marginal biotite-granite and an inner biotite-muscovite-granite."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Intrusive margin of the pluton against surrounding contact metamorphosed rock."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H3949 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DE> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.105e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DE> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/P> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Geophysical studies indicate that it extends to a depth of 10 - 12km. (Parslow, 1968; Parslow and Randall, 1973)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Not applicable."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DE> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.943e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Cairnsmore of Fleet Granite [Obsolete: use COFG]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:GALGR ;
    skos:definition "Granite and trace aplitic microgranite. Igneous intrusive bedrock. Occurs onshore. Emplaced during the Emsian Age (Devonian Period) (410.5-394.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:COFG1 ,
        namedrockunit:COFG2 ;
    skos:notation "COFG"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cairnsmore of Fleet Pluton"@en ;
.

