        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SEATM"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "It comprises grey, laminated mudstones and siltstones with yellow-grey, fine- to medium-grained, planar bedded and cross-stratified sandstones. Trough cross-bedded channel-fill sandstones are also present. Plant fragments, plant rootlets and drifted wood casts are common, together with occasional, thin coals, and grey mudstone seatearths. Dinosaur footprints are occasionally present."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The lower boundary is conformable or disconformable at the gradational upward transition from white or grey, fine- to medium-grained cross-bedded sandstone of the Moor Grit Member, to the predominantly level-bedded siltstone, mudstone or thin, cross-laminated sandstone with plant fragments of the Long Nab Member (Nami and Leeder, 1978; Livera and Leeder, 1981; Powell et al., 1992)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1036 ,
        namedrockunit:H2909 ,
        namedrockunit:H4766 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JB> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.709e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "It ranges in thickness from 23 to 28 m on the North Yorkshire Coast 24 to 33 m inland in the Hambleton Hills. It thins south-westwards towards the Market Weighton High and is between 5 to  10 m thick in the Howardian Hills."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is conformable or disconformable, at the boundary between siltstone, mudstone or cross-bedded sandstone with plant fragments of the Long Nab Member (Scalby Fm.) and the Cornbrash Formation, which at Cayton Bay [TA 078 841], comprises red-brown, sandy, nodular, bioturbated limestone with oysters and other bivalves; u-shaped burrows extend down from the base of the Cornbrash Formation into the underlying grey siltstone and silty sandstone of the Scalby Formation (Wright, 1977). Inland in the Hambleton Hills and Howardian Hills, the Cornbrash Formation (limestone) is generally absent (Powell et al., 1992, p.53) and the upper boundary is taken at the base of the yellow-brown, calcareous medium-grained sandstone with bivalves (Kellaways Rock Member, Osgodby Formation). The Kellaways Rock Memmber is referred to as the Redcliff Rock Member by Page (1989)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.653e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Level Bedded Unit [Obsolete: use LNAB]"@en ,
        "Upper Deltaic Series (part) [Obsolete: use LNAB]"@en ,
        "Upper Estuarine Series (part) [Obsolete: use LNAB]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:SCY ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and trace coal, sandstone, seat-earth mudstone and siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Bajocian Age - to - Bathonian Age (Jurassic Period) (170.9-165.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "LNAB"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Long Nab Member"@en ;
.

