    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 6 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Either seen as a transition from calcareous sandstones and sands, commonly glauconitic (Glauconitic Beds), to limestone of Portland Stone Member, which is probably diachronous, or as a sharp, non-conformable junction with generally non-calcareous and commonly ferruginous sands and sandstones of the Lower Greensand, or Whitchurch Sand Formations, or by calcareous mudstones of the Gault Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JI> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.431e+02 ;
