namedrockunit:GOLS
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent
        namedrockunit:DPCG ,
        namedrockunit:DRBC ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The formation typically comprises variegated, mottled mudstone with occasional thin fine-grained sandstone. However, it becomes more sandy towards the south-east of its distribution (e.g. 12/28-1). The mudstones are green, brick red, light grey and yellow, but they become predominantly red-brown with depth. They are occasionally calcareous, variably silty and fissile. Rootlet horizons, highly laminated bituminous mudstone and limestone stringers occur. Sandstones are upward-fining, cemented by authigenic silica, sometimes shelly and display a variety of planar and ripple cross-bedded structures. Clasts of reworked limestone occur near the base of the formation and may be laterally equivalent to the Dunrobin Conglomerate Member onshore."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base is characterised by a downwards change from mudstone or interbedded sandstone and mudstone to microcrystalline limestone with chert inclusions (Stotfield Calcrete Formation). Where the Stotfield Calcrete Formation is absent (e.g. Well 12/23-1) the boundary is placed at a downward change from dominantly mudstone to dominantly sandstone lithology (Lossiehead Formation). A marked downward decrease in average gamma-ray values and an increase in average velocity occurs at the base of the Golspie Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H5105 ,
        namedrockunit:H5106 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/TR> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.057e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 60 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is characterised by a downward passage from white to pale grey sandstone with siltstone (Mains Formation) to variagated mudstone (Golspie Formation). An increase in average gamma values and a decrease in velocity generally coincides with the boundary."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JH> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.995e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Dullan Member [Obsolete: use GOLS]"@en ,
        "Dunrobin Castle Member"@en ,
        "Dunrobin Pier Conglomerate Member"@en ,
        "Varicoloured Unit of the Dunrobin Bay Formation [Obsolete: use GOLS]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:DUNR ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone with subsidiary sandstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Rhaetian Age (Triassic Period) - to - Hettangian Age (Jurassic Period) (205.7-199.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "GOLS"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Golspie Formation"@en ;
.

