namedrockunit:HMSF
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent
        namedrockunit:S164 ,
        namedrockunit:S535 ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PESST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Onshore, predominantly yellowish aeolian sandstones, some deposited by sheet floods; fine- to coarse-grained, with well rounded grains. Basal beds are pebbly. Large-scale, crossbedded or laminated; rarely contorted. Some beds friable, others hard and siliceous. Contains fossil reptiles. \r
\r
Offshore, the Hopeman Sandstone Formation is largely composed of clean sandstones, and is characterized by low gamma-ray values, commonly between 20 and 35 API units. The sandstones are white, pale grey or red, and moderately to well sorted. Grain size is mainly fine or medium, but intervals of coarse, pebbly sandstone have been encountered in southernmost wells. The sandstones have silica, carbonate or anhydrite cements, and are only locally argillaceous. Reddish brown and greenish grey mudstones are interbedded with the sandstones in well 12/26-1; elsewhere they occur only as sporadic partings. Some wells have recorded a few thin beds of pale brown limestone that may be of calcrete type."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """Onshore, the unconformity with the Devonian Upper Old Red Sandstone. \r
\r
Offshore, the Hopeman Sandstone Formation rests on carbonate, anhydrite, or clastics-dominated Upper Permian sections in different areas of the western Inner Moray Firth. Where the Upper Permian is composed of clastic sediments (Bosies Bank Formation), the boundary is defined by a sharp downward change from sandstone to interbedded mudstone and sandstone (e.g. 11/30-6), marked by a sharp downward increase in gamma-ray values, resistivity and velocity. The facies transition is equally sharp above the carbonate and anhydrite sections."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1597 ,
        namedrockunit:H4710 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/PUL> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 2.595e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The formation is locally more than 70 m thick onshore (Clemmensen, 1987) (E.g. 61 m, Clarkly Hill No. 1 Borehole [NJ 1270 6833]). Offshore, the formation extends over an area of about 4000 km2 of the Inner Moray Firth; it is generally more than 75 m thick and has its maximum recorded thickness of 165 m in block 12/27."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The Hopeman Sandstone Formation is conformably overlain by the Lossiehead Formation throughout the western Inner Moray Firth and by the Burghead Sandstone Formation in coastal outcrops. No marked lithological contrast takes place across the boundary in either setting, but, in offshore sections, the top of the formation is marked by a sharp downward decrease in gamma-ray values."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/TE> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 2.467e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Cutties Hillock Sandstone"@en ,
        "Hopeman-Cummingstown Sandstone"@en ,
        "Sandstones of Cutties Hillock (Quarry Wood) [Obsolete: use HMSF]"@en ,
        "Sandstones of Hopeman [Obsolete: use HMSF]"@en ;
    skos:broader
        namedrockunit:HERO ,
        namedrockunit:NRS ;
    skos:definition "Sandstone with subsidiary mudstone and pebbly (gravelly) sandstone and trace limestone and siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs both onshore and offshore. Deposited during the Lopingian Epoch (Permian Period) - to - Early Triassic Epoch (Triassic Period) (259.5-246.7 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "HMSF"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hopeman Sandstone Formation"@en ;
.

