namedrockunit:LORS
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/D> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """[Obsolete: use DAUG] \r
\r
The coarsening-upwards succession comprises red floodplain mudrocks, with common calcrete, and sandstones ranging from high-sinuosity channelised deposits to low-sinuosity, braided stream deposits, the latter including sheet flood sandstones and laterally accreted sandbodies. These represent increasingly proximal alluvial deposition, culminating in coarse conglomerate fan deposits locally."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the group is placed at the appearance of red beds in the late Silurian. In the Marloes peninsula of south-west Pembrokeshire, the base of the group is placed at the conformable junction of the tidal deposits of the Gray Sandstone Group and the overlying Red Cliff Formation. Elsewhere, the base of the group is traditionally placed at the base of the Ludlow Bone Bed, a thin, lenticular phosphatised lag deposit at the base of the littoral Downton Castle Sandstone Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DL> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.19e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DL> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The Lower Old Red Sandstone Group is up to 4200 m thick in the Pembroke peninsula and up to about 2000 m thick elsewhere."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is a major regional unconformity, above which Upper Devonian rocks of the Upper Old Red Sandstone Group overstep the Lower Old Red Sandstone Group."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/DL> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.943e+02 ;
    lexicon:isObsoleteEquivalentOf
        namedrockunit:AUCR ,
        namedrockunit:DAUG ,
        namedrockunit:LCHA ,
        namedrockunit:LKSF ,
        namedrockunit:LNK ,
        namedrockunit:WHIL ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/deprecated> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Auchencrow Burn Sandstone Formation"@en ,
        "Daugleddau Group"@en ,
        "Lamancha Conglomerate Formation"@en ,
        "Lanark Group"@en ,
        "Lanark Sandstone Formation [Obsolete: use SWAS]"@en ,
        "White Hill Sandstone Formation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Obsolete name. Sandstone with subsidiary conglomerate, mudstone and siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Early Devonian Epoch (Devonian Period) (419-394.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "LORS"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lower Old Red Sandstone Group [Obsolete: use DAUG]"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:LTA
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/O> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DALTUF"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/VCSST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Dacitic lapilli-tuff, typically lithic rich with both silicic and mafic clasts; interpreted as welded ignimbrite; locally with thin tuff and volcaniclastic sandstone interbeds. Component members are recognised in some areas."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Base of dacitic lapilli-tuff. Unconformably overlies erosion surface on top of Seathwaite Fell Sandstone Formation (volcaniclastic sandstone). Basaltic andesite and andesite sills may intervene locally."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H4772 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/O4> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 4.582e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/O4> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "170 to more than 800m"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Base of overlying formations: Esk Pike Formation (mudstone and siltstone) and, in the southeast of the district, Tarn Hows Tuff Formation (andesitic tuff and lapilli)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/O4> ;
