namedrockunit:TYWI
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/Q> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/P"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/Z"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Unconsolidated, stratified gravels, sands, silts and clays forming the alluvial deposits of the River Towy (Afon Tywi) and its tributaries. Includes contemporaneous head, colluvium and pedogenic deposits within the catchment area. Clasts principally consist of local, Lower Palaeozoic turbidites, admixed to the west of Llandovery with Old Red Sandstone clasts. Aggradational river terraces and major alluvial fans are dominated by pebble cobble gravels and medium- to coarse-grained sand; the modern floodplain is domiated by silt and clay, and locally includes lenses of peat."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Sharp, unconformable and locally channelised, predominately on bedrock and Late Devensian head and Welsh glacigenic deposits (e.g Brecknockshire and Plynlimon till formations). Locally beyond the ice limit of the Late Glacial Maximum (e.g. north and west of Cwmduad [SN 3748 3125], it might overlie older alluvial, periglacial and glacial deposits. Interdigitates in a complex manner with Tidal Flat Deposits (Gwynllwg Formation of Bowen, 1999) in the estuary."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:ALF ,
        namedrockunit:ALV ,
        namedrockunit:HEAD ,
        namedrockunit:LDE ,
        namedrockunit:RTD ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QH> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.18e-02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QH> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Generally to about 10m; it might be locally thicker in the lower reaches of the Tywi."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Generally the present ground surface or, locally, overlain by peat."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/QH> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 0e+00 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Alluvial fan deposits"@en ,
        "Alluvium"@en ,
        "Head"@en ,
        "Lacustrine deposits"@en ,
        "River terrace deposits"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:WWACA ;
    skos:definition "Clay, gravel, sand and silt and trace peat. Stratified superficial deposits. Occurs onshore. Formed during the Holocene Epoch (Quaternary Period) (.0118-0 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "TYWI"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tywi Valley Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:UPR
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/V"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Upnor Formation is typically composed of variably but commonly abundant glauconitic fine- to coarse-grained sand with variable clay and silt content, and with beds, lenses and stringers of well-rounded, black flint gravel, and minor thin clays, commonly interbedded with glauconitic sand laminae and lenses. When fresh, the sands are dark grey-brown to dark green depending on proportion of glauconite grains which may be more than 25%. In south-east London there is a persistent pebble bed at the top. The sands weather pale grey-brown to yellow-brown but the glauconite remains dark green. In the central and northern London Basin pedogenic processes during deposition of the overlying Reading Formation modified the sands, resulting in development of carbonate concretions, clay enrichment and colour mottling that exceptionally extend through the formation. Oysters are common in unweathered sections. \r
\r
The Upnor Formation represents proximal marine sedimentation in predominantly high energy, partly tidally influenced environments. In contrast to that of the Thanet Formation, the heavy mineral content of the sands suggests a provenance from the south (Morton, 1982). \r
\r
Paleocene: Late Thanetian (NP9), based on calcareous nannofossil assemblages, dinoflagellate cysts and magnetostratigraphy (Ali and Jolley, 1996; Ellison et al., 1996; King, in prep.; Knox, 1996)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """In the eastern part of the London Basin, the Upnor Formation rests on silty fine-grained sand at a burrowed contact with the Thanet Formation, with burrows extending as much as 0.5 m into the Thanet Formation, or on a channelled erosion surface (King, in prep). The Upnor Formation is likely to be composed of medium to coarse-grained sand that is greener and commonly more gravelly than that of the Thanet Formation but in places has a confusingly similar lithology, especially where, as in the far east of the London Basin, bioturbation has produced a gradational junction (Ellison et al., 1994). According to Morton (1982), the top of the Thanet Formation has been leached during a period of exposure, probably with some erosion, prior to deposition of the Upnor Formation. \r
\r
In the west of the London Basin, to the west of the Thanet Formation outcrop and subcrop, the Upnor Formation rests on a burrowed contact with chalk and flints of the Chalk Group, commonly modified by karstic dissolution. \r
\r
In East Anglia, the Upnor Formation rests on a burrowed contact with the clay of the Ormesby Clay Member."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:BBD ,
        namedrockunit:H5097 ,
        namedrockunit:H5099 ,
        namedrockunit:HAC ,
        namedrockunit:RFBB ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 5.92e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GT> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 15 m in the east of the London Basin. Typically 5 to 8 m in central London but locally as little as 2 m, generally decreasing westwards to less than 2 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition """In general, marked by an upwards change from green glauconitic sand or sandy gravel to colour-mottled clays of the Reading Formation, or dark shelly clay or lignitic sand of the Woolwich Formation. Commonly however the Upnor Formation  has been affected by contemporary pedogenic alteration associated with the mid-Lambeth Group Hiatus. Its original character may then be obscured and the contact with the overlying Reading Formation unclear. \r
\r
In north and east Kent, the Lambeth Group (including the top of the Upnor Formation) was eroded prior to deposition of the Harwich Formation (Oldhaven Member)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GT> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 5.6e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Bottom Bed [Obsolete: use UPR]"@en ,
        "Hales Clay Member [Obsolete: use ORW, UPR]"@en ,
        "Reading Bottom Bed [Obsolete: use UPR]"@en ,
        "Reading Formation Basement Bed [Obsolete: use UPR]"@en ,
        "Woolwich Bottom Bed [Obsolete: use UPR]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:LMBE ;
    skos:definition "Clay and sand with subsidiary gravel. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Thanetian Age (Palaeogene Period) (59.2-56 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "UPR"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Upnor Formation"@en ;
.

