namedrockunit:HTHE
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "The formation comprises medium to dark grey to brown mudstone and siltstone, predominantly, with sporadic thin sandstone stringers or concretions of pale brown limestone or dolomitic limestone. The mudstone is usually poorly fissile, soft to firm and moderately calcareous (due to the common calcareous microfossils). The mudstone is locally carbonaceous and pyritic. The limestone is generally microcrystalline and hard producing a low gamma and high velocity spikes. The sandstone stringers are usually friable, fine-grained and quartzose; locally with glauconite and mica. Thicker sandstone bodies, which are given member status (Bruce, Freshney and Ling sandstone members), are mainly very fine and fine-grained, sometimes micaceous and occasionally calcite cemented. A spiculitic sandstone occurs in the Inner Moray Firth (Alness Spiculite Member)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The nature of the lower boundary varies as the formation overlies a number of different units. It is usually recognised by a downward transition from mudstone and siltstone (Heather Formation) to sandstone or interbedded sandstone, siltstone and coal (Hugin, Tarbert or, in the Inner Moray Firth, Beatrice formations). The lower boundary is marked by a down-section decrease in gamma and velocity values. In the Outer Moray Firth and Central Graben the downward transition from mudstone to thinly interbedded sandstone, mudstone and coal (Stroma Member of the Pentland Formation) or red and grey-brown Triassic mudstone (Smith Bank Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.682e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "East Shetland Basin: up to 350 m. South Viking Graben: up to 600 m. Central North Sea: up to 700 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Mudstone of the Kimmeridge Clay overlies mudstone of the Heather Formation, so that the boundary is difficult to locate lithologically. It is usually taken at a sharp down-section decrease in gamma values, sometime associated with a decrease in resistivity and increase in velocity. However, in basinal sections there are a number of geophysical excursions that could be confused with the boundary. Often there is a down section lightening of the colour of the mudstones at the boundary, a decease in organic content and an increasein the proportion of thin limestone beds. Locally, the Brae, Fulmar or Piper formations overlie the Heather Formation and in this case the boundary can be recognised by the downward change from sandstone to mudstone."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JO> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.548e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:HMBG ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and siltstone with subsidiary sandstone and trace limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs offshore. Deposited during the Bathonian Age - to - Oxfordian Age (Jurassic Period) (168.2-154.8 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:ALNS ,
        namedrockunit:BUSA ,
        namedrockunit:FAIR ,
        namedrockunit:FESA ,
        namedrockunit:GORS ,
        namedrockunit:LISA ;
    skos:notation "HTHE"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Heather Formation"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:HTL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/C> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DOLMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DSLST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DSMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Mid grey, predominantly thick-bedded, fine- to coarse-grained, burrowed, skeletal packstones, with thin beds and partings of shaly dolomite mudstone and siltstone. Some thin-bedded dark grey bituminous skeletal packstones interbedded with thin argallaceous wackestones and mudstones. Coarse, locally cross bedded, peloidal/skeletal/oolitic packstones/grainstones developed in lower and upper parts in northern sections. On the southeast crop of the South Wales Coalfield the Formation is predominantly dolomitized."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base is taken at the sharp erosional contact between the thin-bedded calcite mudstones and the mudstones of the underlying Caswell Bay Mudstone Formation and the overlying coarse peloidal/skeletal/oolitic packstone/grainstones forming the lower part of the Formation in South Wales and the Weston-super-Mare areas. A basaltic lava is present in the lower part of the sequence in the Weston-super-Mare area. In the southern parts of the Welsh outcrop, the Formation overlies the Gully Oolite Formation. Here the base is taken at the sharp erosional contact between the oolitic grainstones of the Gully Oolite Formation and the overlying coarse skeletal packstone/grainstones of the Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:BBL ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CJ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.445e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CJ> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "55m on the south crop of the South Wales Coalfield, thickening southwards to 133m in west/central Vale of Glamorgan."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the formation is gradational and taken at the incoming of a predominantly oolitic grainstone sequence [Hunts Bay Oolite Subgroup] above the skeletal packstones or skeletal/pelioidal/oolitic packstone/grainstones of the Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CJ> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.43e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Birnbeck Limestone Formation [Obsolete: use HTL]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:PEMB ;
    skos:definition "Limestone with subsidiary dolomitic limestone and trace dolomitic silicate-mudstone and dolomitic siltstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Arundian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (344.5-343 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:FCM ,
        namedrockunit:FHL ,
        namedrockunit:PANTL ,
        namedrockunit:SCLA ,
        namedrockunit:WORRL ;
    skos:notation "HTL"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "High Tor Limestone Formation"@en ;
.

