        "Bowhills Formation [Obsolete: use EN]"@en ,
        "Calcareous Conglomerate Group [Obsolete: use EN]"@en ,
        "Enville Beds [Obsolete: use EB, EN]"@en ,
        "Enville Formation [Obsolete: use EN]"@en ,
        "Hamstead Formation [Obsolete: use EN]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:SAL ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and sandstone with subsidiary conglomerate and pebbly (gravelly) sandstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Asturian Substage (Carboniferous Period) - to - Cisuralian Epoch (Permian Period) (310-274.4 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:notation "EN"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Enville Member"@en ;
.

namedrockunit:FE
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/ARLMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CALSST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CAMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SALMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SHMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SMCCST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Silicate-mudstone, grey, bedded, variably calcareous, grading to lime-mudstone, fossiliferous, with units of thinly interbedded, more or less silici-muddy limestone (including the Fuller's Earth Rock Member, Dodington Ash Rock Member), and well-bedded sandy limestone and calcareous sandstone (including the Eyford Member and Througham Member). Where the Fuller's Earth Rock Member is present, the mudstone beds below and above are termed the Lower Fuller's Earth Member and Upper Fuller's Earth Member respectively, and parts of these may be given local names (see separate entries). The commercial fuller's earth bed occurs in the upper part of the formation in the Bath area. In the English Channel the formation is predominantly mudstone with carbonaceous material at some levels and shell beds. In the St George's Channel  it is predominantly mudstone but includes calcareous sandstone beds."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "In the northeast Cotswolds, passage up from fine- to medium-grained ooid-grainstone of the Chipping Norton Limestone Formation (Great Oolite Group) into silicate-mudstone, or (Cotswolds to the English Channel) generally sharp, commonly non-sequential, junction between predominantly coarse-grained peloidal, ooidal and shell-fragmental limestones or (St George's Channel) calcareous sandstone of the Inferior Oolite Group overlain by silicate-mudstone."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H2180 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.682e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "5 to over 260 m (thickest in the Wessex Basin area). Offshore: generally over 100 to 300 m in the English Channel (Hamblin et al., 1992; Barton et al., 2011), up to 300 m in the Celtic Sea - St George's Channel (Tappin et al., 1994)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Generally sharp, commonly non-sequential, junction between variably calcareous silicate-mudstone, and limestones of overlying parts of Great Oolite Group: including (from Bath south to Dorset and in the English Channel) fossiliferous limestones of the Wattonensis Beds of the Frome Clay Formation, and (northeast of Bath) ooidal and shell-fragmental limestones, including the Chalfield Oolite Formation, Athelstan Oolite Formation, Taynton Limestone Formation, or fine-grained limestone of the Tresham Rock Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JN> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.653e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Fuller's Earth [Obsolete: use FRC, FE]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:GOG ;
    skos:definition "Calcareous mudstone, limestone and mudstone with subsidiary calcareous sandstone, calcilutite and sandy limestone and trace shelly mudstone and smectite-claystone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs both onshore and offshore. Deposited during the Bathonian Age (Jurassic Period) (168.2-165.3 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
