namedrockunit:CHNT
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/J> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Five lithofacies have been described: \r
\r
1) Bioturbated, fossiliferous marine mudstones and siltstones with thin, poorly sorted, very fine grained, glauconitic sands at their base. \r
\r
2) Bioturbated, silty, fine-grained sandstones with fragments of bivalves and belemnites. \r
\r
3) Well sorted, massive to cross-bedded, bioturbated, medium-grained sandstones with sporadic shell debris. \r
\r
4) Well sorted, planar to cross-bedded, coarse-grained sandstones. \r
\r
5) Upward fining, very coarse- to coarse-grained, cross-bedded sandstones. \r
\r
Locally, argillaceous and bioturbated sandstones with very high gamma-ray values ('Hot Sands member' of Waddams and Clark, 1991) occur in the upper part of the member. \r
\r
In many wells the lithofacies of the Chanter Sandstone Member are stacked in upward-coarsening cycles (e.g. well 15/16-6) (Turner et al., 1984). In the type section there are three large scale upward coarsening cycles and two upward fining cycles occur (Richards et al., 1993). \r
\r
The mudstones within the member are commonly dark grey, carbonaceous and micaceous. In the Ivanhoe and Rob Roy fields, the basal unit comprises organic-rich mudstones with pyritic nodules, passing up into bioturbated mudstones and siltstones grading up into argillaceous sandstones (Parker, 1991). \r
\r
In the Piper Field Maher (1981) and Schmitt and Gordon (1991) recognised seven subunits in the Chanter Sandstone Member, informally designated 'C' to 'I' in descending order."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base of the Chanter Sandstone Member, where it rests on the Pibroch Sandstone Member, is placed at the base of a widespread marine mudstone informally known as the 'I Shale' (of Maher, 1981). Where the Pibroch Sandstone Member is missing, the base is placed either at the base of the Kimmeridgian shallow marine sandstone section, or at the base of a thin unit of Kimmeridge Clay mudstone (the 'I Shale')."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H5153 ,
        namedrockunit:H5154 ,
        namedrockunit:H5155 ,
        namedrockunit:H5156 ,
        namedrockunit:H5157 ,
        namedrockunit:H5158 ,
        namedrockunit:H5159 ,
        namedrockunit:H5160 ,
        namedrockunit:H5161 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JD> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.548e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JD> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 300 m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top is commonly defined by the down-section change from mudstones or siltstones to sandstones. Wireline log signatures often show a transitional change across the boundary, but on structural highs it may be sharp. The top of the member can be difficult to define from wireline log data. This is particularly so when the Claymore Sandstone Member overlies the Chanter Sandstone Member (e.g. in well 15/12-1), although in some areas a thin unit of Kimmeridge Clay separates the two."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JD> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.492e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
