namedrockunit:EIGL
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/BREC"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/HAW"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MUG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/OLBA"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/TUF"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/VCSD"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Eigg Lava Formation comprises the sequence of lavas that crops out on Eigg and Muck and, as down-faulted slivers, in south-east Rum (Allwright, 1980; Emeleus, 1997). \r
\r
The lowest units, exposed in north and north-east Eigg, are tuffs, typically only a few cm thick. The majority of the flows are of alkali olivine basalt and hawaiite. Mugearites occur especially about 50 m above the base of the formation, and near the top there is a distinctive group of feldspar-phyric basaltic hawaiite flows. Interspersed with the lavas are thin, massive, red to orange mudstones and siltstones, some of which are of volcaniclastic origin. \r
\r
The lowest units on Muck comprise an assemblage of laterally impersistent olivine basalt, feldspar-phyric basaltic hawaiite, hawaiite and mugearite lavas and sedimentary deposits. The higher flows are mainly of olivine basalt but with a flow of feldsparphyric basalt near the summit of Beinn Airein. Bright red to orange mudstones are commonly interleaved with the lavas, especially near the base of the succession. Some represent transported material, whereas others grade downwards into reddened flow tops and most likely developed by in-situ lateritic weathering. A coarse deposit, termed the Camas Mór Breccia, crops out beneath shoreface boulders at the east end of Camas Mór. It is dominated by clasts, generally up to 30 cm across, of grey and brown limestone (Duntulm (DTM) and Kilmaluag (KML) formations, respectively), in a matrix consisting of much smaller clasts of black carbonaceous siltstone and grey and brown impure limestone. \r
\r
On SE Rum, metamorphosed and fractured basalt, intruded by north-west-trending basaltic dykes, occurs as a fault-bound mass, about 1 km in length, within the Main Ring Fault on the south-east slopes of Beinn nan Stac, and on the north-east side of Dibidil (Emeleus, 1997)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The lavas on Eigg lie unconformably on the Upper Cretaceous Strathaird Limestone Formation (STRL) and the Middle Jurassic Great Estuarine Group (GEST). The lavas on Muck overlie the Middle Jurassic Kilmaluag Formation (KML) at Camas Mór. On Rum, the basalt unconformably overlies Lower Jurassic Broadford Beds (BFB) (Smith, 1985)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GP> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 6.6e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GP> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The volcanic succession on Eigg is about 400 m thick and dips gently towards the south-west. The volcanic succession on Muck comprises about 140 m of near-horizontal or gently north-west­dipping lavas."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GP> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 5.6e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:RUSM ;
    skos:definition "Hawaiite, mugearite and olivine-basalt with subsidiary breccia, mudstone, tuff and volcaniclastic-sediment. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Paleocene Epoch (Palaeogene Period) (66-56 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
