namedrockunit:LULW
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent
        namedrockunit:LPB ,
        namedrockunit:LWB ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CARN"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Dominated in the lower part by pale-weathering, ostracod-rich calcarenites that are commonly rippled. Stromatolitic or algal, micritic limestone, with evaporitic material, is also common (particularly in east Dorset). The upper part of the formation comprises monotonous, interbedded dark grey mudstones and white-weathering micrites. Detrital quartz is common at several levels. The Lulworth Formation comprises three members in Dorset, namely in ascending order the Mupe Member, the Ridgway Member and the Worbarrow Tout member. Elsewhere the formation is undivided or is itself incorporated into an undivided Purbeck Limestone Group. The formation includes the informal Lower Purbeck Beds and the lower part of the Middle Purbeck Beds (up to the base of the Cinder Bed) of the traditional scheme. Within the Weald an equivalent of the Cinder Bed has been identified thus allowing the succession there to be considered within the formal terminology."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The boundary is marked by the first occurrence of finely laminated, ostracod-rich limestones above the more massive, shelly limestones of the Portland Limestone."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:LPB ,
        namedrockunit:LWB ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JI> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.492e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "In Dorset, 27 to 63m. In the central Weald area boreholes show the formation to be about 86m thick (Broadoak Borehole TQ 62 SW/4 [6195 2214]). In the Vale of Wardour in Wiltshire the formation is up to 15m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the formation is taken at the top of the Cherty Freshwater Bed, comprising thick-bedded, chert-rich micrites. Overlain by the Cinder Bed at the base of the Durlston Formation."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KR> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.377e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Lower Purbeck Beds [Obsolete: use LULW]"@en ,
        "Lulworth Beds [Obsolete: use LULW, OKY]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:PB ;
    skos:definition "Calcarenite and limestone with subsidiary mudstone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Tithonian Age (Jurassic Period) - to - Berriasian Age (Cretaceous Period) (149.2-137.7 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:narrower
        namedrockunit:BSL ,
        namedrockunit:MUP ,
        namedrockunit:MURI ,
        namedrockunit:RID ,
