namedrockunit:RB
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent
        namedrockunit:REA ,
        namedrockunit:WPF ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/C"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/S"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Dominantly clay or silty clay, colour-mottled by pedogenetic processes in a humid environment (Buurman, 1980), together with lensoid channel-fill bodies of bioturbated or cross-bedded fine- to coarse-grained sand. In the clays, red hues dominate with a more or less equal proportion of blue-grey and brown. Red to purple sediments decrease towards the east where there were shorter periods of subaerial weathering and pedogenesis, and reducing conditions in ephemeral lagoons. Beds of dark grey or black clay occur locally: these may be of significance for regional correlation at the interface between the upper and the lower parts of the Reading Formation. The proportion of sand beds varies greatly, and may constitute more than half the formation, dominantly in the basal part (e.g. Crane and Goldring, 1991; Ellison and Williamson, 1999, fig. 4). Fine-grained, bioturbated to laminated glauconitic sand with Ophiomorpha burrows is present in the Reading area. Gravelly sands with informational mud-flake breccias have been referred to as a marginal 'Lane End facies' by Daley (1999). See also White (1906). In parts of London, the upper part of the Lower Mottled Clay includes calcrete as diffuse nodules or as a more continuous layer of limestone. \r
\r
The Reading Formation is considered to have been deposited mainly in various fluvial environments within a coastal flood-plain. However, Ellison et al. (1994) point out that primary sedimentary structures in the Reading Formation clays have been largely obscured, mainly by pedogenic processes, and that it is possible that some of the Reading Formation sediments originated in estuarine or other marginal marine environments. \r
\r
Early Eocene (earliest Ypresian), possibly including the latest Paleocene (Thanetian), by reference to magnetostratigraphy and stable isotope stratigraphy."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """In the London Basin, the base of the Reading Formation is marked by the upwards change from typically green, glauconitic variably clayey gravelly sands of the Upnor Formation to vari-coloured mottled clays, locally with brownish sands of the Reading Formation. In places, the boundary has been obscured by contemporary pedogenic processes, including the downwards translocation of clay and pigmentation (Ellison et al., 1994; Page and Skipper, 2000). \r
\r
In the Hampshire Basin, the Reading Formation rests unconformably on chalk."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H2293 ,
        namedrockunit:H480 ,
        namedrockunit:MCL ,
        namedrockunit:WRB ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 5.92e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Up to 39 m (in the Guildford district, Ellison et al., 2002), generally 12 to 20 m. Occurs in two leaves in Central London. In the Farringdon area, the Lower Mottled Clay is mostly between 2 and 4 m thick, but locally more than 5 m, whereas the Upper Mottled Clay is mostly between 4 and 8 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Reading Formation is generally sharply defined, at an upward change from vari-coloured mottled clay to glauconitic sands and sandy clays of the Harwich Formation, or to sandy clays of the Walton Member (London Clay Formation), or to shelly grey clays of the Upper Shelly Clay (possible Brixton Formation)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/GY> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 4.81e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Mottled Clay (Reading Formation) [Obsolete: use RB]"@en ,
