namedrockunit:INV
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/DOLO"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMSTN"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Inverclyde Group (Paterson and Hall, 1986) represents continental and peritidal facies. \r
\r
In the Midland Valley of Scotland it comprises, in ascending order, the Kinnesswood, Ballagan and Clyde Sandstone formations. \r
\r
In Northern England, in the Berwick area and Solway Basin, the Kinnesswood and Ballagan formations are separated by the Kelso and Birrenswark Volcanic formations respectively (see Greig, 1988 and Lumsden et al., 1967). There is no equivalent to the Clyde Sandstone Formation of the Midland Valley of Scotland in the Northern England Province. \r
\r
The group is characterised by sandstones with pedogenic carbonate (continental fluvial clastic 'cornstone' subfacies) and by silty mudstones containing thin beds of dolostone and limestone (peritidal marine and evaporite 'cementstone' subfacies). There may be some lateral passage of the two subfacies. \r
\r
Tournaisian (Famennian? to Chadian) in age. \r
\r
The continental fluvial clastic 'cornstone' subfacies represents alluvial fan, fluvial channel and floodplain overbank, deposited in a semi-arid climate. \r
\r
The peritidal marine and evaporite 'cementstone' subfacies represents alluvial plain and marginal marine flats subject to periodic desiccation and fluctuating salinity, in a semi-arid climate (Waters et al., 2007)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """In the Midland Valley of Scotland, the base of the group is taken at the base of the Kinnesswood Formation where the dominantly sandstone lithologies of the underlying Stratheden Group (Late Devonian: defined by Paterson and Hall, 1986) are succeeded by pedogenic carbonate-bearing strata. Typically transitional, the base is locally defined by an unconformity in the west of the Midland Valley. \r
\r
In Northern England the base of the group is taken where Silurian strata (greywackes with interbedded mudstones) of the Riccarton Group are succeeded unconformably by red sandstones, siltstones and conglomerates of the Kinnesswood Formation."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:CMST ,
        namedrockunit:CSM ,
        namedrockunit:SLG ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CF> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.593e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription """In the Midland Valley of Scotland, the Inverclyde Group is about 1500 m thick. \r
\r
It is up to 900 m thick in the Tweed Valley, Northumberland, and up to 640 m thick in the Solway Firth area."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition """In the Midland Valley of Scotland, the base of the Strathclyde Group (heterolithic clastic and nonmarine carbonate facies) defines the top of the Inverclyde Group, and the youngest formation, the Clyde Sandstone Formation, that itself represents a return to fluviatile pedogenic facies similar to the basal Kinnesswood Formation. \r
\r
In Northern England, the base of the Border Group (heterolithic clastic and nonmarine carbonate facies and fluviodeltaic 'Millstone Grit' facies) defines the top of the group."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CI> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.445e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Calciferous Sandstone Measures [Obsolete: use INV, SYG]"@en ,
        "Cementstone Group [Obsolete: use INV, BGN, CMNT]"@en ,
        "Stirling Group [Obsolete: use INV]"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sandstone and silty mudstone with subsidiary dolostone, limestone and nodular limestone (cornstone). Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Courceyan Substage - to - Chadian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (359.3-344.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
    skos:isTopConceptOf lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
