        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PEL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PSAMM"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SEMPEL"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Dominated by calcitic limestone, in part slumped, resedimented; however, east of mid-Deeside the limestone is replaced by psammite and quartzite with thin beds of calcsilicate rock; lavas, hyaloclastites and graphitic pelites present in Tayvallich area; Banffshire Coast - thick semipelite and calc sequence in upper part."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Bottom of lowest limestone bed in Loch Tay Limestone Formation where present; from Glen Clova to Deeside change from dominant semipelite to psammite or quartzite with calcareous beds."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H202 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1e+03 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/UG> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Generally 100-250m, but in Aberdeenshire and Banffshire where semipelitic and psammitic lithologies come in, it may reach 2.5km"@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Top of highest limestone bed in Loch Tay Limestone Formation where present; in Glen Clova and Glen Esk below unit with beds of strongly magnetic semipelite, Banffshire Coast - incoming of dominantly turbiditic psammite and semipelite."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/AZ> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 5.41e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "Tayvallich Lavas [Obsolete: use DBTV]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:ARGY ;
    skos:definition "Calcsilicate-rock, limestone, psammite and semipelite with subsidiary basalt and trace pelite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Neoproterozoic Era (Precambrian) (1000-541 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
