namedrockunit:NPCH
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/K> ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CAMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CHLK"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/FLNT"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Principally blocky, white firm to moderately hard chalk with numerous marls or paired marl seams. Flint occurs sporadically in the upper part in the deeper basin areas of the Southern Province. In some localities flint, in seams, occurs towards the base of the formation most notable over structural highs, towards the margins of the outcrop and within the \"Transitional\" Province."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The lower boundary is conformable at the bedding plane immediately below the Gun Gardens Main Marl (the Lulworth Marl of Gale, 1996) in the standard Sussex succession. The mapping boundary is placed at the highest shell-detrital chalk and at the incoming of sporadic flints. In the \"Transitional\" Province the top of these shell-detrital chalks is at the Odsey Marl (see Hopson et al., 1996)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H1729 ,
        namedrockunit:H2341 ,
        namedrockunit:H4223 ,
        namedrockunit:NPBE ,
        namedrockunit:NPUH ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 9.39e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/F> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Typically between 35 and 50 m but  is as little as 10 to 25 m thick in Wiltshire, north Hampshire and Berkshire over synsedimentary structural highs. The formation expands in the \"Transitional\" Province where it is thought to be up to 75 to 80 m thick."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition """The top is conformable at the base of Glynde Marl 1 in Sussex, but one of the higher marls elsewhere although invariably in the interval Glynde Marls to Southerham Marls in the Southern Province. The mapping boundary is placed at the appearance of nodular chalks and significant flint development within that range of marls. \r
\r
In the "Transitional" Province the formation expands in response to the later inception of nodularity and hardground development (characteristic of the Lewes Nodular Chalk Formation) such that the upper boundary is diachronous here and placed below the "Chalk Rock Member" at the Reed Marl (the lateral equivalent of the Bridgewick Marls of the Southern Province)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KT> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 8.94e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Aycliff Member [Obsolete: use NPCH]"@en ,
        "New Pit Beds [Obsolete: use NPCH]"@en ,
        "New Pit Chalk Member [Obsolete: use NPCH]"@en ,
        "Shakespeare Cliff Member [Obsolete: use HCK, NPCH]"@en ,
        "Upper Holywell Beds [Obsolete: use NPCH]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:WHCK ;
    skos:definition "Chalk with subsidiary calcareous mudstone and trace flint. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Turonian Age (Cretaceous Period) (93.9-89.4 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
