        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/FLNT"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Principally limestones (hardened chalks) with subordinate flint, marl, sponge bed, hardground, conglomeratic and fossil acme bands, with glauconite grains where formations become condensed over structural highs."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The base rests disconformably on various members of the Hibernian Greensands Formation and oversteps (unconformable) onto older pre-Cretaceous rocks. Internal overstep within the group, principally at the level of the Post-Larry Bane White Limestone Subgroup (informal term defined withiin this Lexicon entry), places successively higher formations at the base of the group over structural highs at the margins of the principal basins"@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H241 ,
        namedrockunit:H596 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KS> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 8.57e+01 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "Highly variable dependant on the position within structurally controlled basins (environments) of which five were named by Fletcher (1977) as the Hebridean Basin (presumably incorporating his Londonderry Shelf and North Antrim Basin), the Highland Border (also termed the Dalradian Massif), the Midland Valley (usually termed the East Antrim Basin) and the Southern Uplands. Up to 150m+ of \"White Limestone\" was encountered in the Aughrimderg Borehole [H 880 685] (Wilson , 1972, p 56) but the thickest and most complete development at outcrop is found on the North Antrim coast where 120m of beds are present, although not at any one locality. The Geological Society Special Report on the Cretaceous  (Rawson et al., 1978) gives a value of 130m."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Eroded contact where the group is overlain by the Palaeogene Antrim Basalts or Quaternary deposits."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KM> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 6.6e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Upper Chalk (White Limestone) and Lower Chalk (Glauconitic Chalk) [Obsolete: use UWLF]"@en ,
        "Upper Chalk [Obsolete: use UWLF, FCK]"@en ;
    skos:definition "Chalk with subsidiary conglomerate, flint and limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Santonian Age - to - Maastrichtian Age (Cretaceous Period) (85.7-66 Ma BP)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lexicon:NamedRockUnit ;
