namedrockunit:CK
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CAMDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CHLK"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/FLNT"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PHOST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Chalk, with or without flint and discrete limestone, marl (calcareous mudstone), sponge, calcarenite, phosphatic, hardground and fossil-rich beds. \r
\r
Upper Cretaceous, Cenomanian to Maastrichtian."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "The lower boundary is generally unconformable (at a burrowed surface) on the underlying Lower Cretaceous strata (Upper Greensand and Gault Formations in the Southern Province, and on the Hunstanton Formation in the Northern Province). Oversteps onto older strata in limited basin marginal situations."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent namedrockunit:H2700 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KE> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.005e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "The thickness of the whole group is variable depending on the degree of post-Cretaceous erosion and the relative development of its constituent formations. Onshore the thickest development is within the Hampshire/Sussex area of the Southern Province, where up to about 560 m of strata are preserved; the most chronostratigraphically complete succession is in Norfolk but this is thought to be only some 400 m thick; within the Northern Province up to 530 m are preserved but the thickest succession is within the North Sea area where about 1000 to 1300 m are preserved."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The upper boundary is unconformable beneath the Palaeogene or Quaternary basal unconformity onshore in the UK.  It is conformable beneath the Danian age Maureen Formation in parts of the North Sea. Elsewhere offshore the contact is unconformable."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/KM> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 6.6e+01 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel "The Chalk [Obsolete: use CK]"@en ;
    skos:definition "Chalk with subsidiary calcareous mudstone, flint and limestone and trace phosphorite. Stratified bedrock. Occurs both onshore and offshore. Deposited during the Cenomanian Age - to - Maastrichtian Age (Cretaceous Period) (100.5-66 Ma BP)."@en ;
