namedrockunit:DN
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/COAL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/CONG"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/PESST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """Sandstone with some conglomerate and pebbly sandstone and sporadic fissile mudstone beds. Some workable coal seams are also present. Sandstone is typically a coarse-grained lithic arenite of 'Pennant' type. \r
\r
The sandstones formed in channels and floodplains within a broad alluvial tract. \r
\r
The lower part of the member is Bolsovian in age. This is constrained in the Somerset Coalfield with the base of the member occurring between the Aegiranum and Cambriense marine bands, whereas in the Bristol Coalfield it occurs above the Cambriense Marine Band. At Bickley Wood [ST 644 703] plant fossils have been interpreted as indicating a lower or middle Asturian age (Cleal and Thomas, 1996)."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Base of member is the base of the lowest mappable lithic sandstone of 'Pennant' type above the grey and red mudstone-dominated succession of the Winterbourne Formation. The base is diachronous; In the Somerset Coalfield the base of the member occurs between the Aegiranum and Cambriense marine bands, wheras in the Bristol Coalfield it occurs above the Cambriense Marine Band."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H4001 ,
        namedrockunit:S144 ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CC> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.1475e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/M> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "About 120 m thick in the northern part of the Bristol Coalfield [ST 78], broadly thickening southward to about 660 m in the Somerset Coalfield [ST 75]. About 275 m in the type area."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "The top of the Downend Member is defined at the top of the Mangotsfield Coal and associated thin fissile mudstone, overlain by the sandstone-dominated succession of the Mangotsfield Member."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CAS> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.075e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Downend Formation [Obsolete: use DN, WINT]"@en ,
        "Downend Group [Obsolete: use DN]"@en ;
    skos:broader namedrockunit:PES ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and sandstone with subsidiary conglomerate and pebbly (gravelly) sandstone and trace coal. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Bolsovian Substage - to - Asturian Substage (Carboniferous Period) (314.75-307.5 Ma BP)."@en ;
