        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SALMST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription "Variety of mudstone-dominated and ooidal, bioclastic and fine-grained limestone formations."@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition "Change up from ooidal and/or pisoidal limestone (Salperton Limestone Formation/Lincolnshire Limestone Formation) or ironstone or ferruginous sandstone (Northampton Sand Formation), all of Inferior Oolite Group; overlain by silicate/calcareous mudstone (Fuller's Earth Formation) or pale sandstone (Horsehay Sand Formation/Stamford Member of Rutland Formation) or sandy, fine-grained ooid limestone (Chipping Norton Limestone Formation). Generally a non-sequence or disconformity."@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:GOL ,
        namedrockunit:H4534 ,
        namedrockunit:H5115 ,
        namedrockunit:RDBN ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JB> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 1.709e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "About 20 m through Lincolnshire and the Midlands (East Midlands Shelf), thickening south through the Cotswolds (60 to 90 m) to 100 to 200 m in Somerset and Dorset, and in the Weald subcrop (Green, 1992; Sumbler, 1996). Offshore English Channel up to 224 m (Hamblin et al., 1992)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "Base of succeeding Kellaways Formation; comprising mudstone, commonly shelly at base, resting generally sharply and conformably or non-sequentially on bioclastic limestone of the Cornbrash."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/JC> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 1.615e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
