    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription "About 110 m thick in the type area; 76 - 152 m thick in South Staffordshire; 70 - 127 m thick in Warwickshire; about 200 m thick in Wyre Forest; 350 m thick in North Staffordshire; remaining thickness about 125 m in the East Midlands and about 200 m in Lancashire."@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "At the base of the the Salop Formation. At outcrop an upward change from grey to orange-red locates the boundary, except where the Halesowen Formation is itself red; in this case the boundary is drawn arbitrarily, above the top of the highest litharenite sandstone. Although in boreholes, the gamma ray log character is used."@en ;
