namedrockunit:MG
    a skos:Concept ;
    lexicon:hasBroaderPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/C> ;
    lexicon:hasLateralEquivalent namedrockunit:MIGR ;
    lexicon:hasLithology
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/COAL"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/FEST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/LMST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/MDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SARCST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SDST"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SEATS"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SISTCC"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/EarthMaterialClass/RockClassAll/SLST"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    lexicon:hasLithologyDescription """The Millstone Grit Group (Millstone Grit facies) is of Namurian age. \r
\r
In Northern England, each of the seven Namurian Stage successions has been assigned a distinct formation name, with the exception of the thin, and usually mudstone-dominated successions of the Chokierian and Alportian, which have been joined to form a single formation. The component formations, in ascending order, are the Pendleton, Silsden, Samlesbury, Hebden, Marsden and Rossendale formations. Where marine bands cannot be recognised, or other biostratigraphical data is absent, it is necessary to leave the group undivided. \r
\r
The Cefn-y-fedw Sandstone Formation of north Wales and the Morridge Formation of Staffordshire and the East Midlands, both of Millstone Grit facies, were deposited along the northern margin of the Wales-Brabant High. Lateral continuity between these two broadly similar formations has not been established. Therefore, distinct formation names are maintained. \r
\r
The Millstone Grit Group comprises broadly upward-coarsening cyclic sequences of sandstone, siltstone and grey mudstone, with subordinate and typically thin coal seams and seatearth (palaeosol) horizons. The base of the cycles are typically marked by thin marine bands of dark grey to black, calcareous, shaly mudstone, with distinct ammonoid faunas. The widespread occurrence of the marine bands makes them of primary stratigraphical importance for correlating the successions."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasLowerBoundaryDefinition """In the northern part of the Central Pennine Sub-basin, the base of the group is taken at the base of the first thick quartz-feldspathic sandstone, of Namurian age, typically present above the Bowland Shale Formation. \r
\r
In the southern part of the Central Pennine Sub-basin, the Cefn-y-fedw Sandstone Formation conformably overlies the Dinantian Minera Formation to the south and locally rests on the Cefn Mawr Limestone in the north. It intertongues with and onlaps the Pentre Chert Formation, and both intertongue with, and are conformably succeeded by, the Bowland Shale Formation. The Morridge Formation conformably overlies, and intertongues with, the Bowland Shale Formation."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasObsoleteEquivalent
        namedrockunit:H2071 ,
        namedrockunit:SH ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CN> ;
    lexicon:hasOldestAgeValue 3.303e+02 ;
    lexicon:hasPredominantAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CN> ;
    lexicon:hasRockUnitRank <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/RockUnitRank/G> ;
    lexicon:hasThicknessDescription """The thickest development is in the northern part of the Central Pennine Sub-basin, where 1225 m is recorded in Wharfedale (Ramsbottom et al., 1978). \r
\r
On the Askrigg Block the group is about 500 m thick, significantly thinner than the equivalent strata present within the Central Pennine Basin to the south."""@en ;
    lexicon:hasUpperBoundaryDefinition "A conformable top, located at the incoming of thick coal-bearing Coal Measures facies, at the top of the Rough Rock, or more precisely at the base of dark grey shaly mudstone of the Subcrenatum Marine Band, with eponymous fauna where present (Earp and others, 1961, p.104)."@en ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAge <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Geochronology/Division/CN> ;
    lexicon:hasYoungestAgeValue 3.19e+02 ;
    dcterms:source "http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    reg:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Millstone Grit Formation (Offshore) [See Also MG]"@en ,
        "Millstone Grit [Obsolete: use COS, MG, CFS]"@en ,
        "Shale Group [Obsolete: use MG]"@en ;
    skos:definition "Mudstone and sandstone with subsidiary coal, ironstone, seat-earth sandstone, silicate-claystone and siltstone and trace ganister and limestone. Stratified bedrock. Occurs onshore. Deposited during the Namurian Stage (Carboniferous Period) (330.3-319 Ma BP)."@en ;
